    "content": "\"Read option Cyclic reduction (PCR) \\\"Parallel cyclic reduction\\\" in /home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/reshape-test/20261019-2344-tree/out/work/seed-1/R1/physical-widener.md. I'm thinking about running something on this with up to $1,000 of GPU credit at Lambda's on-demand prices (https://lambda.ai/pricing). Make it concrete: what to build, the baseline, how to check it's right, and the cost. Cite a URL for every factual claim. Write to /home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/reshape-test/20261019-2344-tree/out/work/seed-1/R1/physical/e01a75ff/producer.md. Option record: {\\\"id\\\":\\\"Cyclic reduction (PCR)\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Parallel cyclic reduction\\\",\\\"summary\\\":\\\"Parallel cyclic reduction solves the block-bidiagonal Newton correction (a tridiagonal/bidiagonal system) in O(log N) steps with N-fold parallelism, dropping the per-iteration depth of PinT methods from O(T) to O(log T) (https://courses.grainger.illinois.edu/cs554/fa2015/notes/09_tridiagonal_8up.pdf).\\\",\\\"root_link\\\":\\\"Bears on Q3, the 'shit ton of GPU parallelism' question, specifically whether width can shorten the cheap sequential part.\\\",\\\"compute_open\\\":\\\"settled: N-fold parallel cyclic reduction is known to solve a tridiagonal system in O(log N) steps instead of O(N), so more parallel compute already buys the O(T)→O(log T) per-iteration depth reduction (https://courses.grainger.illinois.edu/cs554/fa2015/notes/09_tridiagonal_8up.pdf).\\\",\\\"sequential\\\":\\\"The block-bidiagonal solve is a forward substitution in which row t waits on row t-1 (O(T) deep per iteration), but the rows need not run in order: cyclic reduction eliminates independent pairs simultaneously, which pays off only when the T equations can be halved each round and the machine has ~T processors to feed them (https://courses.grainger.illinois.edu/cs554/fa2015/notes/09_tridiagonal_8up.pdf).\\\",\\\"source_urls\\\":[\\\"https://courses.grainger.illinois.edu/cs554/fa2015/notes/09_tridiagonal_8up.pdf\\\"]}. Root question: Can one long sequential simulation, like a single MCMC chain or a rainfall-runoff model stepped through decades of daily rainfall, be parallelised over time by guessing the whole trajectory and then correcting every step at once with Newton iterations, and does that only work when the system forgets its past? Is compute a major blocker at some level, and could that be fixed with a shit ton of GPU parallelism?. Read only /home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/reshape-test/20261019-2344-tree/out/work/seed-1/R1/physical-widener.md, /home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/reshape-test/20261019-2344-tree/out/work/seed-1/R1/framer.md, pages you fetch and what you write under /home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/reshape-test/20261019-2344-tree/out/work/seed-1/R1/physical/e01a75ff; write only under /home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/reshape-test/20261019-2344-tree/out/work/seed-1/R1/physical/e01a75ff. When the plan is written, return it as the record in your output schema; producer.md stays your working file. In the record, put every number the cost or the size of the test depends on in numbers, each with a basis: CITED (a page states this number for this same setting; give the page text that shows it), PROXY (a page states it for a different model, GPU, dataset, size or component; give the page text and say what differs), MEASURED (you ran code: save the script and its output under /home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/reshape-test/20261019-2344-tree/out/work/seed-1/R1/physical/e01a75ff/pilot/ and give the output line and the machine), DERIVED (an expression over other ids), DESIGN (your choice), or GUESS (no source; say what it rests on). Give each rate as the per-GPU price and the GPU count, and each line's hours as an expression. A line's hours are wall-clock hours of the whole instance, not GPU-hours: 40 GPU-hours on an 8-GPU instance are 5 hours. The default line is a time box: DESIGN hours at a rate from the prices below, ended by a timer or stop rule; it needs no estimate of the work inside it, but if you have one, put it in need. Use GUESS only where a number drives the plan. For each PROXY or GUESS the cost or result depends on, name the phase that measures it in measured_by, and give that phase's threshold as gate if your plan changes on it. Optional: scenarios (pessimistic or sensitivity cases, as values for the inputs they change), optional lines for stretch work, instead_of for fallbacks, and yields (the number ids that say what the money buys: cases, samples, primes). Refer to the budget as budget_usd and to the price sheet's filesystem price as fs_usd_gib_month. List human time and money outside Lambda in outside_budget. predictions.if_lever and predictions.if_not are the outcomes you expect, on the same measured quantity, if more compute does or does not change the answer. predictions.noise_rule says how you will tell a real difference from run-to-run noise. predictions.expected is the outcome you expect, if_lever, if_not or unsure, with one sentence why. scored_against says what the measured outcome is compared with to decide it is correct. Each source carries the exact sentence from the page that you rely on. Cite pages a reader can open without a login or paywall: a value that appears only in a paywalled full text cannot be checked, so take it from an open source (an open-access version, a preprint or a public database). In restructuring, sequential_part is the part of this computation that one answer waits on step after step, and how many steps; method is why those steps need not run in order and how your plan computes them at the same time instead, or \\\"none\\\" if the plan only runs independent work side by side or splits each step across GPUs. If method is not none, also give condition, what must be true of this system for the method to pay off and the cheap measurement that checks it; condition_phase, the id of the phase that makes that measurement before the