    "content": "Found 100 matches (more matches available)\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14d3773d7001gWdv6cbvfRnCrf:\n  Line 21: [![NCBI home page](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/pd-medc-pmc-cloudpmc-viewer/production/9b1d9d87/var/data/static/img/ncbi-logos/nih-nlm-ncbi--white.svg)](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/)\n\n  Line 73: ![Royal Society of Chemistry logo](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/banners/logo-rscsd.png)\n\n  Line 81: -   [View in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=\"Digit%20Discov\"[Title%20Abbreviation])\n\n  Line 158: Thermophysical properties such as the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of organic molecules are important to the performance of liquid formulations such as lubricants and refrigerants.[1](#cit1) Accurate prediction of these properties for pure organic molecules is also important for a wide range of industries, including energy, chemicals, and pharmaceuticals.[2–4](#cit2) In this context, the lack of reliable thermophysical data is one of the largest stumbling blocks in rational molecular,[5](#cit5) formulation,[6](#cit6) and process[7](#cit7) design. In particular, lubricants are required to adhere to strict viscosity limits across a wide temperature range (40–100 °C);[8](#cit8) however, for the organic molecules relevant to this application (C10–C50),[9](#cit9) viscosity–temperature data are still lacking. Viscosity data are available for many normal alkanes up to ∼C24, but data for branched alkanes, cycloalkanes, aromatics, alkyl cycloalkanes, and alkyl aromatics is much more sparse.[10](#cit10) There is also a lack of reliable thermophysical data available for oxygen-containing molecules such as esters, ethers, and glycols in the C10–C50 range.[11](#cit11) It is often possible to interpolate between missing entries with calculated predictions using a reliable equation of state, such as the perturbed-chain statistical associating fluid theory (PC-SAFT).[12,13](#cit12) Several other predictive models are available for pure hydrocarbons,[14](#cit14) with the most promising being group contribution and particularly entropy scaling methods.[15,16](#cit15) Quantitative structure–property relationship (QSPR) models[17–21](#cit17) have also been identified that are capable of predicting the viscosity of organic liquids. Within the chemical families and conditions for which they are parameterized, these methods can be highly accurate; their main limitation is reduced transferability, with predictive performance degrading outside the molecules and conditions fo...\n  Line 170: ## Methodology\n\n  Line 174: To construct a robust and diverse dataset for model development and evaluation, we collected reference data for density, thermal conductivity and dynamic viscosity at atmospheric pressure and at both 40 °C and 100 °C. Only molecules with a dynamic viscosity of approximately 1–50 mPa s at 40 °C were included, because this is the range accessible to equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations.[39](#cit39) Data were compiled from the Landolt–Börnstein series[73](#cit73) and the NIST Chemistry WebBook[74](#cit74) (accessed June 2024), resulting in 835 unique molecules carrying approximately 4700 critically evaluated reference values after the structure canonicalization, unit conversion and deduplication steps described below.\n\n  Line 178: For interpolation of the liquid density values, the NIST WebBook uses the Tait equation.[77](#cit77) For liquid thermal conductivity, the TransportPolynomial model was applied, utilizing temperature-dependent polynomial fits to experimental thermal conductivity data. For the Landolt–Börnstein series,[73](#cit73) cubic spline interpolation using a minimum of three surrounding data points was employed to estimate the property values at the desired temperatures. In cases where only the kinematic viscosity was reported, it was converted to dynamic viscosity using *µ* = *ν* × *ρ*, where *µ* is dynamic viscosity, *ν* is kinematic viscosity, and *ρ* is density. Molecules whose viscosity was reported only as kinematic and for which no density was available at the same temperature were excluded (283 molecules at each temperature). Canonicalization and deduplication of the 1187 source entries give 1114 unique molecules, of which 835 retain at least one usable label and form the dataset used here. The viscosity values were normalized by taking their natural logarithm to improve regression performance during ML training.[54](#cit54)\n\n  Line 258: Dimensionality-reduced embeddings provide a compact alternative to high-dimensional molecular descriptors by reducing noise and redundancy while retaining the most informative features.[81](#cit81) In this study, embeddings generated using principal component analysis (PCA) and uniform manifold approximation and projection (UMAP) were evaluated across a range of dimensionalities to assess their impact on model performance, as both the choice of method and retained dimensionality can influence predictive accuracy. PCA and UMAP embeddings were used as inputs to ML models to compare linear and nonlinear dimensionality reduction strategies for molecular property prediction. A detailed analysis of embedding method and dimensionality effects, including root-mean-square error (RMSE) trends across properties and model types, is provided in the SI. Embeddings were generated using the scikit-learn and UMAP-learn libraries from *z*\\-score normalized molecular descriptors.\n\n  Line 270: All models were evaluated under a single shared nested cross-validation protocol, applied to molecular representation configurations that were fixed in advance. The embedding dimensionality and the fingerprint radius and bit size were chosen by grid search on a single 80 : 10 : 10 train/validation/test split, as reported in the SI, so the protocol is nested with respect to hyperparameter selection and conditional on those representation configurations. The dataset was partitioned into five fixed outer folds, so that both temperatures of a given molecule always fall in the same fold and no molecule appears in more than one outer test set. The folds were constructed by stratified sampling over quintiles of the log-transformed 40 °C viscosity labels (molecules without a viscosity label were assigned *via* median-filled strata), then frozen. For each outer fold, hyperparameters were selected by random search over 25 configurations sampled from model-specific ranges, scored on a designated validation partition of the non-test data (approximately 85 : 15 train/validation); the selected configuration was then retrained on all non-test data and evaluated once on the untouched outer test fold. Full details of the architectures, hyperparameter ranges, and training procedures for each model are provided in the SI.\n\n  Line 284: The protocol suggested by Schmitt *et al.*[26](#cit26) was adopted in the EMD simulations because it has been shown to enable accurate calculation of the viscosity of lubricant-sized molecules. A velocity–Verlet integrator with a 1.0 fs timestep was employed.[109](#cit109) Bonds containing hydrogen atoms were constrained with the SHAKE algorithm.[110](#cit110) Constraining C–H bonds is important for obtaining accurate thermal conductivities with all-atom force fields, as it suppresses the classically over-excited high-frequency C–H stretching modes that otherwise inflate the Green–Kubo heat flux.[111](#cit111) Lennard-Jones interactions were truncated at 12 Å with long-range tail corrections applied to energy and pressure, and long-range electrostatics were treated using the particle–particle particle–mesh (PPPM) method[112](#cit112) with a relative accuracy of 10−5. Geometric mixing rules were used for the unlike-atom interactions, and intramolecular 1–4 interactions were scaled by 0.5 following the OPLS convention.\n\n  Line 332: XGBoost and RF models, when used with molecular descriptors, performed most consistently across all three transport properties. XGBoost with descriptors gave the lowest viscosity error of any combination (RMSE = 0.35 and 0.30 in ln(*η*/mPa s) at 40 and 100 °C, *R*2 = 0.86 and 0.83) and was among the best two or three combinations for density and thermal conductivity, while RF with descriptors gave the lowest density error (RMSE = 0.047 g cm−3). An error of 0.35 on the logarithmic scale corresponds to a typical discrepancy of a factor of 1.4 between predicted and measured viscosity. This aligns with previous studies,[124,125](#cit124) which also found tree-based models to be reliable across datasets. These methods handle nonlinear relationships and high-dimensional features effectively, making them suitable for the diverse information captured by molecular descriptors, and are relatively insensitive to feature scale, irregular target functions and uninformative features.[126](#cit126)\n\n  Line 452: E.O. thanks the Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (EPSRC) for PhD funding through their Doctoral Training Partnership (DTP) scheme (EP/T51780X/1). D.D. thanks the Royal Academy of Engineering (RAEng) for support through the Shell/RAEng Research Chair in Complex Engineering Interfaces. J.P.E. thanks the RAEng for support through their Research Fellowships scheme. We also acknowledge support from the Shell University Technology Centre (UTC) for Mobility and Lubricants and the EPSRC InFUSE Prosperity Partnership (EP/V038044/1). We acknowledge the use of Imperial College London Research Computing Service ([https://doi.org/10.14469/hpc/2232](https://doi.org/10.14469/hpc/2232)) and the UK Materials and Molecular Modelling Hub, which is partially funded by the EPSRC (EP/T022213/1, EP/W032260/1 and EP/P020194/1). We thank Stefan J. Eder (TU Wien) for useful discussions.\n\n  Line 488: 27.  Moore J. D. Cui S. T. Cochran H. D. Cummings P. T. Rheology of lubricant basestocks: A molecular dynamics study of C30 isomers. J. Chem. Phys. 2000;113:8833–8840. doi: 10.1063/1.1318768. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1318768)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=J.%20Chem.%20Phys.&title=Rheology%20of%20lubricant%20basestocks:%20A%20molecular%20dynamics%20study%20of%20C30%20isomers&author=J.%20D.%20Moore&author=S.%20T.%20Cui&author=H.%20D.%20Cochran&author=P.%20T.%20Cummings&volume=113&publication_year=2000&pages=8833-8840&doi=10.1063/1.1318768&)\\]\n\n  Line 545: 84.  Weininger D. SMILES, a Chemical Language and Information System: 1: Introduction to Methodology and Encoding Rules. J. Chem. Inf. Comput. Sci. 1988;28:31–36. doi: 10.1021/ci00057a005. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1021/ci00057a005)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=J.%20Chem.%20Inf.%20Comput.%20Sci.&title=SMILES,%20a%20Chemical%20Language%20and%20Information%20System:%201:%20Introduction%20to%20Methodology%20and%20Encoding%20Rules&author=D.%20Weininger&volume=28&publication_year=1988&pages=31-36&doi=10.1021/ci00057a005&)\\]\n\n  Line 583: 122.  Dysthe D. K. Fuchs A. H. Rousseau B. Fluid transport properties by equilibrium molecular dynamics. I. Methodology at extreme fluid states. J. Chem. Phys. 1999;110:4047–4059. doi: 10.1063/1.478286. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.478286)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=J.%20Chem.%20Phys.&title=Fluid%20transport%20properties%20by%20equilibrium%20molecular%20dynamics.%20I.%20Methodology%20at%20extreme%20fluid%20states&author=D.%20K.%20Dysthe&author=A.%20H.%20Fuchs&author=B.%20Rousseau&volume=110&publication_year=1999&pages=4047-4059&doi=10.1063/1.478286&)\\]\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14beec4af001BbAEaEt1BIHJ90:\n  Line 17:  [![arXiv logo](/static/base/1.0.1/images/arxiv-logo-primary-light.svg) Back to arXiv](/)\n\n  Line 96: The design of functional materials with desired properties is essential in driving technological advances in areas like energy storage, catalysis, and carbon capture [Zhao et al. (2020)](#bib.bib1); [Zhao et al. (2019)](#bib.bib2); [Osman et al. (2021)](#bib.bib3). Generative models provide a new paradigm for materials design by directly generating entirely novel materials given desired property constraints. Despite recent progress, current generative models have low success rate in proposing stable crystals, or can only satisfy a very limited set of property constraints [Xie et al. (2022)](#bib.bib4); [Zhao et al. (2023)](#bib.bib5); [Kim et al. (2020)](#bib.bib6); [Long et al. (2021)](#bib.bib7); [Zheng et al. (2023)](#bib.bib8); [Yang et al. (2023)](#bib.bib9); [Noh et al. (2019)](#bib.bib10); [Court et al. (2020)](#bib.bib11); [Antunes et al. (2023)](#bib.bib12); [Mila AI4Science et al. (2023)](#bib.bib13). Here, we present MatterGen, a model that generates stable, diverse inorganic materials across the periodic table and can further be fine-tuned to steer the generation towards a broad range of property constraints. To enable this, we introduce a new diffusion-based generative process that produces crystalline structures by gradually refining atom types, coordinates, and the periodic lattice. We further introduce adapter modules to enable fine-tuning towards any given property constraints with a labeled dataset. Compared to prior generative models, structures produced by MatterGen are more than twice as likely to be novel and stable, and more than 15 times closer to the local energy minimum. After fine-tuning, MatterGen successfully generates stable, novel materials with desired chemistry, symmetry, as well as mechanical, electronic and magnetic properties. Finally, we demonstrate multi-property materials design capabilities by proposing structures that have both high magnetic density and a chemical composition with low supply-chain risk. We believe that the qu...\n  Line 102: The rate at which we can discover better materials has a major impact on the pace of technological innovation in areas such as carbon capture, semiconductor design, and energy storage. Traditionally, most novel materials have been found through experimentation and human intuition, which require long iteration cycles and are limited by the number of candidates that can be tested. Thanks to the advance of high throughput screening [Curtarolo et al. (2013)](#bib.bib14), open material databases [Jain et al. (2013)](#bib.bib15); [Curtarolo et al. (2012)](#bib.bib16); [Kirklin et al. (2015)](#bib.bib17); [Choudhary et al. (2020)](#bib.bib18); [Talirz et al. (2020)](#bib.bib19), machine-learning-based property predictors [Xie and Grossman (2018)](#bib.bib20); [Chen et al. (2019)](#bib.bib21), and machine learning force fields (MLFFs)[Unke et al. (2021)](#bib.bib22); [Chen and Ong (2022)](#bib.bib23), it has become increasingly common to screen hundreds of thousands of materials to identify promising candidates [Jun et al. (2022)](#bib.bib24); [Rosen et al. (2022)](#bib.bib25); [Zhong et al. (2020)](#bib.bib26); [Merchant et al. (2023)](#bib.bib27). However, screening-based methods are still fundamentally limited by the number of known materials. The largest explorations of previously unknown crystalline materials are in the orders of 10610^{6}–10710^{7} materials [Shen et al. (2022)](#bib.bib28); [Chen and Ong (2022)](#bib.bib23); [Schmidt et al. (2022)](#bib.bib29); [Merchant et al. (2023)](#bib.bib27), which is only a tiny fraction of the number of potential stable inorganic compounds (101010^{10} quaternary compounds only considering stoichiometry [Davies et al. (2016)](#bib.bib30)). Moreover, these methods cannot be efficiently steered towards finding materials with target properties.\n\n  Line 122: We formulate learning a generative model for inverse materials design as a two-step process, where we first pre-train a general base model for generating stable, diverse crystals across the periodic table, and then we fine-tune this base model towards different downstream tasks. In this section, we focus on the ability of MatterGen’s base model to generate stable, diverse materials, which we argue is a prerequisite for addressing any inverse materials design task. Since diversity is difficult to measure directly, we resort to quantifying MatterGen’s ability to generate S.U.N.materials, and provide an additional analysis of the quality and diversity of generated structures. To train the base model, we curate a large, diverse dataset comprising 607,684 stable structures with up to 20 atoms recomputed from the Materials Project (MP)[Jain et al. (2013)](#bib.bib15) and Alexandria[Schmidt et al. (2022)](#bib.bib46); [Schmidt et al. (2022)](#bib.bib29) datasets, which we refer to as Alex-MP-20. We consider a structure to be stable if its energy per atom after relaxation via DFTis below the 0.1 eV/atom threshold of a reference dataset comprising 1,081,850 unique structures recomputed from the MP, Alexandria, and Inorganic Crystal Structure Database (ICSD)datasets. We refer to this dataset as Alex-MP-ICSD. We consider a structure to be novel if it is not contained in Alex-MP-ICSD. We adopt these definitions throughout unless stated otherwise. More details are in [Appendices C](#A3 \"Appendix C Dataset generation ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\") and [D.3.1](#A4.SS3.SSS1 \"D.3.1 RMSD, stability, uniqueness and novelty ‣ D.3 Generating stable and diverse materials ‣ Appendix D Results ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\").\n\n  Line 144: The symmetry of a material directly affects its electronic and vibrational properties, and is a determining factor for its topological [Tang et al. (2019)](#bib.bib54) and ferroelectric [Smidt et al. (2020)](#bib.bib55) characteristics. The generation of S.U.N.materials with a given symmetry is a challenging task, as the symmetric arrangement of atoms in space is hard to enforce without resorting to explicit constraints based on already known materials. Here, we assess MatterGen’s ability to generate S.U.N.materials with a target symmetry by fine-tuning it on space group labels. We choose 14 space groups at random from the subset of space groups that had at least 1000 entries in the training dataset, two for each of the seven crystal systems, and generate 256 structures per space group. The results are shown in [Fig. 4](#S2.F4 \"In 2.4 Designing materials with target symmetry ‣ 2 Results ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\")(a). On average, the fraction of generated S.U.N.structures that belong to the target space group is 20%, and still surpassing 10%10\\\\% for some of the most highly symmetric space groups that were chosen, e.g., P63/mmc\\\\text{P6}\\_{3}/\\\\text{mmc} and Im​3¯\\\\text{Im}\\\\bar{3}. This is a notable result given that most previous generative models struggled in generating highly symmetric crystals [Xie et al. (2022)](#bib.bib4); [Jiao et al. (2023)](#bib.bib56). In [Fig. 4](#S2.F4 \"In 2.4 Designing materials with target symmetry ‣ 2 Results ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\")(b), we show four randomly generated S.U.N.structures from different space groups. Additional details and results are provided in [Section D.5](#A4.SS5 \"D.5 Designing materials with target symmetry ‣ Appendix D Results ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\").\n\n  Line 156: There is an enormous need for new materials with improved properties across a wide range of real-world applications, e.g., for designing carbon capture technologies, solar cells, or semiconductors [Jun et al. (2022)](#bib.bib24); [Rosen et al. (2022)](#bib.bib25); [Zhong et al. (2020)](#bib.bib26). The classical screening-based approach starts from a set of candidates and selects the ones with the best properties. However, screening methods are unable to explore structures beyond the set of known materials. Here, we demonstrate MatterGen’s ability to directly generate S.U.N.materials with target constraints on three different single-property inverse design tasks. These feature a diverse set of properties—magnetic, electronic, and mechanical—with varying degrees of available labeled data for fine-tuning the model. In the first task, we aim to generate materials with high magnetic density, a prerequisite for permanent magnets. We fine-tune the model on 605,000 structures with DFTmagnetic density labels (calculated assuming ferromagnetic ordering) and then generate structures with a target magnetic density value of 0.20​Å−30.20~\\\\text{\\\\AA}^{-3}. Second, we search for materials with a specific electronic property. We fine-tune the model on 42,000 structures with DFTband gap labels, then sample materials with a target calculated band gap value of 3.0 eV. Finally, we target structures with high bulk modulus—an important property for superhard materials. We fine-tune the model on only 5,000 labeled structures, and sample with a target value of 400 GPa. While the tasks above were chosen to evaluate the generality of the model, we note that additional follow-up investigations would be required to assess the suitability of these materials for specific applications, e.g., a superhard material needs to satisfy additional constraints such as a high shear modulus, or a permanent magnet needs a suitable magnetic order and critical temperature. See [Section D.6](#A4.SS6 \"D.6 Desig...\n  Line 254: Diffusion timestep\n\n  Line 258: Number of time discretization steps for the diffusion process\n\n  Line 266: Single-step diffusion transition kernel\n\n  Line 284: log⁡p𝜽​(𝑨0|𝑿t,𝑳t,𝑨t,t)\\\\log p\\_{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}({\\\\bm{A}}\\_{0}|{\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{A}}\\_{t},t)\n\n  Line 286: Predicted logits for atom types at t\\=0t=0.\n\n  Line 353: Diffusion models ([Ho et al., 2020](#bib.bib42); [Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015](#bib.bib64); [Song and Ermon, 2019](#bib.bib41); [Austin et al., 2021](#bib.bib65)) are a class of generative models that learn to revert a diffusion process. The diffusion process (also called the *forward* process) gradually corrupts an input sample 𝒙0{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0} via transition kernels q⁡(𝒙t|𝒙t−1)q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t-1})11 1 We follow the convention in machine learning literature that the functional forms of (conditional) probability density functions depend on the variables that appear as arguments. For example, q⁡(𝒙t|𝒙t−1)q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t-1}) could be written as q𝑿t|𝑿t−1​(𝒙t|𝒙t−1)q\\_{{\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t-1}}({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t-1}) to make the dependence of the functional form on tt explicit, but we avoid this to prevent clutter., defining a Markov chain 𝒙0→𝒙1→⋯→𝒙T{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0}\\\\rightarrow{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{1}\\\\rightarrow\\\\cdots\\\\rightarrow{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{T}, where T∈ℕT\\\\in\\\\mathbb{N} is the number of diffusion steps and 1≤t≤T1\\\\leq t\\\\leq T. Here, we cover the typical case where the data is continuous-valued and the transition kernels are normal distributions. See [Section A.5](#A1.SS5 \"A.5 Atom type diffusion ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\") for details on discrete diffusion models.\n\n  Line 355: The transition kernels are of the general form q⁡(𝒙t|𝒙t−1)\\=𝒩⁡(f⁡(𝒙t−1,t),σt2​𝑰)q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t-1})=\\\\mathcal{N}(f({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t-1},t),\\\\sigma\\_{t}^{2}{\\\\bm{I}}), where f⁡(𝒙t−1,t):ℝd→ℝdf({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t-1},t):\\\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\\\rightarrow\\\\mathbb{R}^{d} is affine in 𝒙t−1{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t-1}. This implies that the one-shot transition kernel q⁡(𝒙t|𝒙0)q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0}) is also Gaussian, and for popular choices f⁡(⋅,t)f(\\\\cdot,t) the mean and variance are known in closed form. This enables us to efficiently obtain a noisy sample 𝒙t{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t} at an arbitrary time step tt during training.\n\n  Line 359: 𝜽∗\\=arg⁡min𝜽​∑t\\=1Tσt2​𝔼q⁡(𝒙0)​𝔼q⁡(𝒙t|𝒙0)​\\[‖𝒔𝜽​(𝒙t,t)−∇𝒙t​log​q​(𝒙t|𝒙0)‖22\\],{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}^{\\*}=\\\\underset{{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}}{\\\\arg\\\\min}\\\\sum\\_{t=1}^{T}\\\\sigma\\_{t}^{2}\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0})}\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0})}\\\\left\\[\\\\|{\\\\bm{s}}\\_{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t},t)-\\\\nabla\\_{{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}}\\\\log q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0})\\\\|\\_{2}^{2}\\\\right\\],\n\n  Line 363: where 𝒔𝜽​(𝒙,t):ℝd×ℝ+→ℝd{\\\\bm{s}}\\_{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}({\\\\bm{x}},t):\\\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\\\times\\\\mathbb{R}\\_{+}\\\\rightarrow\\\\mathbb{R}^{d} is called the *score model*. It is standard practice ([Ho et al., 2020](#bib.bib42); [Song and Ermon, 2019](#bib.bib41)) to parameterize the model to predict the *noise* ϵt\\=−σt​∇𝒙t​log⁡q⁡(𝒙t|𝒙0)\\\\epsilon\\_{t}=-\\\\sigma\\_{t}\\\\nabla\\_{{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}}\\\\log q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0}) instead of the score, since the magnitude of ϵt∼𝒩⁡(𝟎,𝑰)\\\\epsilon\\_{t}\\\\sim\\\\mathcal{N}({\\\\bm{0}},{\\\\bm{I}}) is independent of the diffusion time step tt.\n\n  Line 433: where \\[𝑸t\\]i​j\\=q⁡(at\\=j|at−1\\=i)\\[{\\\\bm{Q}}\\_{t}\\]\\_{ij}=q(a\\_{t}=j|a\\_{t-1}=i) is the Markov transition matrix at time step tt. Cat⁡(𝒂,𝒑)\\\\mathrm{Cat}({\\\\bm{a}};{\\\\bm{p}}) is a categorical distribution over one-hot vectors whose probabilities are given by the row vector 𝒑{\\\\bm{p}}. Similar to DDPM, D3PMassumes that the forward diffusion factorizes over all discrete variables of a data point, i.e., all atomic species are diffused independently with the same transition matrices 𝑸t{\\\\bm{Q}}\\_{t}. Hence, we only consider individual one-hot vectors in this section. D3PMsare trained by optimizing a variational lower bound:\n\n  Line 437: −𝔼q⁡(𝒂1|𝒂0)logp𝜽(𝒂0|𝒂,11)+DKL\\[q(𝒂T|𝒂0)||q(𝒂T)\\]\\\\displaystyle-\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{q({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{1}|{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0})}\\\\log p\\_{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|{\\\\bm{a}}{{}\\_{1}},1)+D\\_{\\\\mathrm{KL}}\\\\left\\[q({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{T}|{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0})\\\\>||\\\\>q({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{T})\\\\right\\]\n\n  Line 445: LCE\\=−𝔼q⁡(𝒂0)\\[∑t\\=2T𝔼q⁡(𝒂t|𝒂0)logp𝜽(𝒂0|𝒂,tt)\\],L\\_{\\\\textnormal{CE}}=-\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{q({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0})}\\\\left\\[\\\\sum\\_{t=2}^{T}\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{q({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0})}\\\\log p\\_{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|{\\\\bm{a}}{{}\\_{t}},t)\\\\right\\],\n\n  Line 453: Three important characteristics of DDPMand DSMare that (1) given 𝒙0{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0} we can sample noisy samples 𝒙t{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t} for arbitrary tt in constant time; (2) after sufficiently many diffusion steps, 𝒙T{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{T} follows a prior distribution that is easy to sample from; and (3) the posterior q⁡(𝒙t−1|𝒙t,𝒙0)q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t-1}|{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0}) in [Eq. 12](#A1.E12 \"In A.5 Atom type diffusion ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\") is tractable and can be computed efficiently. D3PMalso has these properties, as we briefly outline in the following:\n\n  Line 492: As the specific flavor of D3PMforward diffusion we employ the masked diffusion process, which has shown best performance in the original study ([Austin et al., 2021](#bib.bib65)) as well as our initial experiments. Following [Austin et al. (2021)](#bib.bib65), we introduce an extra atom species \\[MASK\\] at index K−1K-1, which is the absorbing or masked state. At each timestep tt, the transition matrices have the particularly simple form\n\n  Line 542: Recall from [Eq. 4](#A1.E4 \"In A.3 Diffusion model background and notation ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\") that training diffusion models requires computing the score function for the one-shot transition kernel. However, for the wrapped normal distribution in [Eq. 19](#A1.E19 \"In A.6 Coordinate diffusion ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\"), (log-)likelihood and score computation are intractable because of the infinite sum. Given the thin tails of the normal distribution, both can be approximated reasonably well with a truncated sum. More specifically, the score function of the isotropic wrapped normal distribution can be expressed as\n\n  Line 544: ∇𝒙¯​log​qσ​(𝒙¯|𝒙)\\=∑𝒌∈ℤ3w𝒌​𝒙¯−𝒙+𝒌σ2,\\\\nabla\\_{\\\\bar{{\\\\bm{x}}}}\\\\log q\\_{\\\\sigma}(\\\\bar{{\\\\bm{x}}}|{\\\\bm{x}})=\\\\sum\\_{{\\\\bm{k}}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{Z}^{3}}w\\_{\\\\bm{k}}\\\\frac{\\\\bar{{\\\\bm{x}}}-{\\\\bm{x}}+{\\\\bm{k}}}{\\\\sigma^{2}},\n\n  Line 558: However, this approach is not suitable for our framework. To see this, note that while in CDVAE the lattice 𝑳{\\\\bm{L}} is fixed during the diffusion of the atom coordinates, we diffuse the lattice simultaneously to the atom coordinates (and atomic species). This makes diffusion of Cartesian coordinates dependent on the lattice diffusion because the wrapped normal’s covariance matrix and periodic boundaries at diffusion timestep tt depend on knowing the lattice matrix 𝑳t{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t}. Consequently, our diffusion process from [Eq. 9](#A1.E9 \"In A.4 Joint diffusion process ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\") no longer factorizes into lattice and coordinates and needs to be adapted:\n\n  Line 568: Here, we need to condition q⁡(𝑿~t+1)q(\\\\tilde{{\\\\bm{X}}}\\_{t+1}) on 𝑳t+1{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t+1} and 𝑳t{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t} because in order to convert the Cartesian coordinates at time step tt to time step t+1t+1 we first need to convert 𝒙~t\\\\tilde{{\\\\bm{x}}}\\_{t} to fractional coordinates using 𝑳t−1{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t}^{-1}, and then to Cartesian coordinates at t+1t+1 using 𝑳t+1{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t+1}.\n\n  Line 616: Thus, in the limit distribution we have a tendency towards cubic lattices 𝑳∝𝑰{\\\\bm{L}}\\\\propto{\\\\bm{I}}, which often occur in nature and have a relatively narrow range of volumes. Further, the lattice vector angles when sampling from the prior are mostly concentrated between 60° and 120°. This aligns both with the range of angles of Niggli-reduced cells as well as the initialization range of cell vector angles in RSS([Pickard and Needs, 2011](#bib.bib69)), suggesting that this is a good starting point for the generation process.\n\n  Line 628: For the atom-type reverse diffusion, recall from [Eq. 16](#A1.E16 \"In Reverse sampling process. ‣ A.5 Atom type diffusion ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\") that we predict the atom types 𝑨0{\\\\bm{A}}\\_{0} given the atom types 𝑨t{\\\\bm{A}}\\_{t} at time tt. For materials, we additionally condition on lattice 𝑳t{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t} and coordinates 𝑿t{\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t}; more precisely, the (unnormalized) log-probabilities log⁡p𝜽​(𝑨0|𝑿t,𝑳t,𝑨t,t)\\\\log p\\_{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}({\\\\bm{A}}\\_{0}|{\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{A}}\\_{t},t) of the atomic species at t\\=0t=0 are computed as:\n\n  Line 630: log⁡p𝜽​(𝑨0|𝑿t,𝑳t,𝑨t,t)\\=𝑯(L)​𝑾,\\\\log p\\_{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}({\\\\bm{A}}\\_{0}|{\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{A}}\\_{t},t)={\\\\bm{H}}^{(L)}{\\\\bm{W}},\n\n  Line 700: The chain-rule-based lattice score predictions from [Eq. 36](#A1.E36 \"In A.8.1 Computation of the predicted lattice scores ‣ A.8 Architecture of the score network ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\") have shown to lack expressiveness for modeling the score of our Gaussian forward diffusion in our early experiments. We hypothesize that this is because our periodic GNNmodel is invariant to the particular choice of unit cell. For instance, it cannot distinguish the two structures in [Fig. 7](#A1.F7 \"In A.8.2 Augmenting the input with lattice information ‣ A.8 Architecture of the score network ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\"). To address this, we drop the invariance of the GNNw.r.t. equivalent choices of the unit cell by injecting information about the lattice angles into the internal representations. This means that the generative distribution is no longer invariant to the concrete choice of unit cell. We nonetheless note that any lattice can be uniquely transformed into its so-called Niggli-reduced cell ([Niggli and Wien, 1928](#bib.bib71)). We apply this transformation to all training data points and, consequently, side-step the loss of cell choice equivariance we introduce. Concretely, we concatenate the roto-translation invariant input edge representations 𝒎inp{\\\\bm{m}}^{\\\\textnormal{inp}} with the cosines of the angles of the edge vectors w.r.t. the lattice cell vectors:\n\n  Line 718: Lcoord\\=∑t\\=1Tσt​(n)2​𝔼q⁡(𝒙0)​𝔼q⁡(𝒙t|𝒙0)​\\[‖𝒔𝒙,𝜽​(𝑿t,𝑳t,𝑨t,t)−∇𝒙t​log​q​(𝒙t|𝒙0)‖22\\],L\\_{\\\\textnormal{coord}}=\\\\sum\\_{t=1}^{T}\\\\sigma\\_{t}(n)^{2}\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0})}\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0})}\\\\left\\[\\\\|{\\\\bm{s}}\\_{{\\\\bm{x}},{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}}({\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{A}}\\_{t},t)-\\\\nabla\\_{{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}}\\\\log q({\\\\bm{x}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{x}}\\_{0})\\\\|\\_{2}^{2}\\\\right\\],\n\n  Line 722: Lcell\\=∑t\\=1T(1−α¯t)​σt​(n)2​𝔼q⁡(𝑳0)​𝔼q⁡(𝑳t|𝑳0)​\\[‖𝒔𝑳,𝜽​(𝑿t,𝑳t,𝑨t,t)−∇𝑳t​log​q​(𝑳t|𝑳0)‖22\\]L\\_{\\\\textnormal{cell}}=\\\\sum\\_{t=1}^{T}(1-\\\\bar{\\\\alpha}\\_{t})\\\\sigma\\_{t}(n)^{2}\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{q({\\\\bm{L}}\\_{0})}\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{q({\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{0})}\\\\left\\[\\\\|{\\\\bm{s}}\\_{{\\\\bm{L}},{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}}({\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{A}}\\_{t},t)-\\\\nabla\\_{{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t}}\\\\log q({\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t}|{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{0})\\\\|\\_{2}^{2}\\\\right\\]\n\n  Line 730: −λCElogp𝜽(𝒂0|𝑿t,𝑳t,𝑨t,t)\\]−𝔼q⁡(𝒂1|𝒂0)\\[logp𝜽(𝒂0|𝑿1,𝑳1,𝑨1,1)\\]\\].\\\\displaystyle-\\\\lambda\\_{\\\\textnormal{CE}}\\\\log p\\_{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|{\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{A}}\\_{t},t)\\]-\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{q({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{1}|{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0})}\\\\left\\[\\\\log p\\_{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|{\\\\bm{X}}\\_{1},{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{1},{\\\\bm{A}}\\_{1},1)\\\\right\\]\\\\Biggr\\].\n\n  Line 768: The conditional score follows from [Eq. 45](#A2.E45 \"In B.2 Classifier-free guidance ‣ Appendix B Fine-tuning the score network for property-guided generation ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\") by taking gradients of the logarithm w.r.t. continuous variables in 𝑴t{\\\\bm{M}}\\_{t}. For example, for fractional coordinates 𝑿t{\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t} we have\n\n  Line 794: We can approximate this guided distribution accordingly with an unconditional and a conditional prediction model, i.e., pθ​(𝒂0|c,𝒂t,t)γ⋅pθ​(𝒂0|𝒂t,t)1−γ≈q~​(𝒂0|c,𝒂t)γ⋅q~​(𝒂0|𝒂t)1−γp\\_{\\\\theta}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|c,{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t},t)^{\\\\gamma}\\\\cdot p\\_{\\\\theta}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t},t)^{1-\\\\gamma}\\\\approx\\\\tilde{q}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|c,{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t})^{\\\\gamma}\\\\cdot\\\\tilde{q}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t})^{1-\\\\gamma}. Taking the logarithm, we obtain\n\n  Line 796: log⁡(pθ​(𝒂0|c,𝒂t,t)γ⋅pθ​(𝒂0|𝒂t,t)1−γ)\\=γ​log⁡pθ​(𝒂0|c,𝒂t,t)+(1−γ)​log⁡pθ​(𝒂0|𝒂t,t).\\\\log\\\\left(p\\_{\\\\theta}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|c,{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t},t)^{\\\\gamma}\\\\cdot p\\_{\\\\theta}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t},t)^{1-\\\\gamma}\\\\right)=\\\\gamma\\\\log p\\_{\\\\theta}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|c,{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t},t)+(1-\\\\gamma)\\\\log p\\_{\\\\theta}({\\\\bm{a}}\\_{0}|{\\\\bm{a}}\\_{t},t).\n\n  Line 819: For crystal structures from MP, we retrieved existing calculations via the MPAPI. For other data sources, we performed new calculations using MPsettings to guarantee consistency of data (see [Section C.2](#A3.SS2 \"C.2 DFT details ‣ Appendix C Dataset generation ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\")). We then followed MP’s data analysis approach as implemented in emmet [Materials Project ()](#bib.bib76), which includes the following steps:\n\n  Line 881: The base unconditional model was trained for 1.74 million steps with a batch size of 64 per GPU over eight A100 GPUs using the Adam optimizer ([Kingma and Ba, 2014](#bib.bib82)). The learning rate was initialized at 0.0001 and was decayed using the ReduceLROnPlateau scheduler with decay factor 0.6, patience 100 and minimum learning rate 10−610^{-6}. For the training loss we use λcoord\\=0.1\\\\lambda\\_{\\\\textnormal{coord}}=0.1 and λcell\\=λtypes\\=1\\\\lambda\\_{\\\\textnormal{cell}}=\\\\lambda\\_{\\\\textnormal{types}}=1, as well as the recommended value λCE\\=0.01\\\\lambda\\_{\\\\textnormal{CE}}=0.01 for the D3PMcross-entropy loss.\n\n  Line 885: For fine-tuning the base model towards different properties, we used a global batch size of 128 and the Adam optimizer. Gradient clipping was applied by value at 0.5. The learning rate was initialized at 6×10−56\\\\times 10^{-5} and we used the same learning rate scheduler as that for the base model. The training was stopped when the validation loss stopped improving for 100 epochs, which resulted in 32,000 - 1.1 million steps depending on the dataset.\n\n  Line 889: For both unconditional and conditional generation, we discretized the reverse diffusion process over the continuous time interval \\[0,1\\]\\[0,1\\] into T\\=1000T=1000 steps. For each time step, we use ancestral sampling (according to [Eqs. 6](#A1.E6 \"In Variance-exploding diffusion ‣ A.3 Diffusion model background and notation ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\"), [8](#A1.E8 \"Equation 8 ‣ Variance-preserving diffusion ‣ A.3 Diffusion model background and notation ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\") and [16](#A1.E16 \"Equation 16 ‣ Reverse sampling process. ‣ A.5 Atom type diffusion ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\")) to sample (𝑿t−1,𝑳t−1,𝑨t−1)({\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t-1},{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t-1},{\\\\bm{A}}\\_{t-1}) given (𝑿t,𝑳t,𝑨t)({\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t},{\\\\bm{A}}\\_{t}) using the score model described in [Section A.8](#A1.SS8 \"A.8 Architecture of the score network ‣ Appendix A Diffusion model for periodic materials ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\"). After each predictor step, one corrector step was applied. We used the Langevin corrector (see Algorithms 4 and 5 in ([Song et al., 2021](#bib.bib43))) for the coordinates 𝑿t{\\\\bm{X}}\\_{t} and the lattice 𝑳t{\\\\bm{L}}\\_{t} with signal-to-noise ratio parameters 0.4 and 0.2, respectively.\n\n  Line 903: Using empirical priors to assess the synthesizability of structures is traditionally done by hand by domain experts and is therefore highly dependent on the chemical intuition and background knowledge of a particular system by the expert. It becomes more difficult for a scientist to evaluate a crystal structure picked “at random”, especially as the number of elements increases, as they are less likely to have encountered this material in their prior work. Some materials are hard to analyze in this way because the prior is simply not known or may be biased; for example, any distribution of a specific property calculated from crystalline materials that have already been synthesized will include bias by not simply including the properties of materials that have not yet been synthesized. These biases can be because certain elements are more abundant, cheaper, or easier to process, or because certain materials have garnered more technological interest, rather than because of an a priori physical reason why those materials could not be made. Simply put, we do not yet know what the distribution of “possible” crystal structures looks like, even within certain constraints (e.g., maximum primitive cell size or number of elements). Therefore, assessing synthesizability using empirical priors is difficult, but might still provide insights. When examining a generated material, in addition to performing a literature review, additional factors can be considered, including:\n\n  Line 942: where 𝒙~n\\\\tilde{{\\\\bm{x}}}\\_{n} indicates the Cartesian coordinates of atom nn, and 𝑷{\\\\bm{P}} is the element-aware permutation operator on the atoms of the generated structure. A lower RMSDindicates that generated structures are closer to their DFT-relaxed counterpart. This in turn reduces the computational time for the DFTrelaxation, which is typically the most costly part of crystal structure generation. Novelty and uniqueness are computed in all model evaluations by comparing the atomic arrangement of every pair of structures that have the same reduced formula and space group via the StructureMatcher utility from the pymatgen Python package [Ong et al. (2013)](#bib.bib79), with the following default parameters: ltol=0.2, stol=0.3, angle\\_tol=5. This definition of novelty is not able to determine whether an ordered structure might be an ordered approximation of a disordered structure, and so some structures might be falsely determined to be novel in this scenario.\n\n  Line 948: Within the 1024 structures generated unconditionally for [Fig. 2](#S2.F2 \"In 2.2 Generating stable, diverse materials ‣ 2 Results ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\")(a), a total of 43 unique, on-hull crystal structures were found: 11 binaries, 22 ternaries, and 10 quaternaries. These are summarized in [Table 2](#A4.T2 \"In D.3.2 Additional qualitative analysis of structures ‣ D.3 Generating stable and diverse materials ‣ Appendix D Results ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\"). Of these, 3 had P1 symmetry, and 3 contained molecules or were molecular crystals. Prototype assignment limited to prototypes available in the robocrystallographer [Ganose and Jain (2019)](#bib.bib91) tool, which will assign the “closest” matching prototype subject to tolerances. The ability for a composition to charge balance assessed by pymatgen and known common oxidation states for each element. As previously discussed, a generated crystal can still be reasonable even if it does not charge balance, and not all materials are ionic.\n\n  Line 1507: To compare the performance of MatterGen against substitution and RSS, we employ an MLFF(MatterSim, see [Section D.1.4](#A4.SS1.SSS4 \"D.1.4 Machine learning force field (MatterSim) details ‣ D.1 Common experimental details ‣ Appendix D Results ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\")) to relax the generated structures, and then perform *ab initio* relaxation and static calculations via DFT(see [Section C.2](#A3.SS2 \"C.2 DFT details ‣ Appendix C Dataset generation ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\") for details). In particular, we perform the following steps: (1) generate structures, (2) relax structures using the MLFF, (3) filter structures for uniqueness, (4) select the 100 structures with lowest predicted energy above hull according to the MLFF, (5) run DFTon these structures. We report metrics only with respect to those structures. To allow for a fair comparison between our generative model and non-generative approaches, we employ the MLFFrelaxation on a greater number of samples for the latter. For RSS, we sample 600,000 structures per chemical system according to the protocol described in [Section D.4.1](#A4.SS4.SSS1.Px1 \"Random structure search details ‣ D.4.1 Additional experimental details ‣ D.4 Generating materials with target chemistry ‣ Appendix D Results ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\"). For substitution, we enumerate every possible structure according to the algorithm detailed in [Section D.4.1](#A4.SS4.SSS1.Px2 \"Substitution details ‣ D.4.1 Additional experimental details ‣ D.4 Generating materials with target chemistry ‣ Appendix D Results ‣ Appendix ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\"), which yields between 15,000 and 70,000 structures per chemical system. For MatterGen, we generate 10,240 structures per chemical system.\n\n  Line 1627: To train the property predictor, we use all materials with DFTVoigt-Reuss-Hill average bulk modulus values from MP[Jain et al. (2013)](#bib.bib15) (including structures with more than 20 atoms), which are 7,108 structures in total. We allocate 80%80\\\\% of the data for the training set, 10%10\\\\% for validation, and 10%10\\\\% for testing. We follow the MatBench benchmark [Dunn et al. (2020)](#bib.bib98) and predict the log10\\\\log\\_{10} bulk modulus. At the end of training, the model achieves a mean absolute error (MAE)of 9.5 GPa9.5\\\\text{\\\\,}\\\\mathrm{G}\\\\mathrm{P}\\\\mathrm{a}. The model was trained using the Adam optimizer. Gradient clipping was applied by value at 0.0.5\\. The learning rate was initialized at 5×10−45\\\\times 10^{-4} and decayed using the ReduceLROnPlateau scheduler with decay factor 0.8, patience 10 and minimum learning rate 10−810^{-8}. The training was stopped when the validation loss stopped improving for 150 epochs.\n\n  Line 1661: -   Osman et al. (2021) Osman, A.I., Hefny, M., Abdel Maksoud, M., Elgarahy, A.M., Rooney, D.W.: Recent advances in carbon capture storage and utilisation technologies: a review. Environmental Chemistry Letters 19(2), 797–849 (2021)\n\n  Line 1695: -   Nouira et al. (2018) Nouira, A., Sokolovska, N., Crivello, J.-C.: CrystalGAN: learning to discover crystallographic structures with generative adversarial networks. arXiv preprint arXiv:1810.11203 (2018)\n\n  Line 