(FoodBERT) and enable finetuning of those features at training time 22 2 We use L2 regularization on the learnable values to avoid the input features going too far from the FoodBERT embeddings. From the results, we observe that leveraging pretrained ingredient features leads to an overall MRR performance that is comparable to the one achieved by learnable randomly initialized embeddings. However, when it comes to OOD results, structural node embeddings appear to bring some performance improvements, with node2vec achieving better results than metapath2vec. Those improvements are even more remarkable when leveraging embeddings from FoodBERT. However, those trends are not preserved ID. Finally, when enabling gradient propagation through FoodBERT, the benefits of generalizing OOD are reduced.\n\n  Line 635: Conclusions. In this paper, we tackled the problem of recipe personalization by decoupling ingredient substitution from recipe editing. We addressed ingredient substitution by introducing a benchmark for the task. We then devised a graph-based ingredient substitution module able to leverage recipe-specific context and generic ingredient relational information encoded as a graph simultaneously to predict plausible substitutions. Finally, we took advantage of the compositionality captured by pretrained language models to integrate the proposed ingredient substitution model within the inverse cooking pipeline, and highlighted the effectiveness of the system to come up with plausible substitutions and adequate recipe modifications. Our research takes one step forward to increase creativity in everyone’s kitchen through personalized tasting experiences.\n\n  Line 722: Finally, we look at some failure modes of the personalized inverse cooking pipeline when used in combination with GISMo, pointing to potential areas of improvement. Figure [11](#A2.F11 \"Figure 11 ‣ Appendix B Visual inspection of recipes ‣ Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\") shows how the creativity of GISMo to come up with ingredient substitutions might be negatively impacted by incomplete, wrong, or near duplicate ingredient predictions of the personalized inverse cooking pipeline. Recall that, in this case, the ingredients predicted by the inverse cooking pipeline constitute the context of GISMo, therefore impacting the kind of substitutions that GISMo can propose. Improving the ingredient prediction step in the personalized inverse cooking pipeline holds the promise to improve GISMo’s suggestions as a result. Moreover, Figure [12](#A2.F12 \"Figure 12 ‣ Appendix B Visual inspection of recipes ‣ Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\") shows the phenomenon of recipe collapse that sometimes occurs when performing (even minor) substitutions with the personalized inverse cooking pipeline. However, as shown in Figure [13](#A2.F13 \"Figure 13 ‣ Appendix B Visual inspection of recipes ‣ Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\"), this phenomenon also appears in the inverse cooking pipeline which does not consider any substitution, pointing to potential areas of improvement of the recipe generator module of the personalized inverse cooking pipeline.\n\n  Line 762: report](/log/2302.07960) [Report  \n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14d399158001BNeCBKeRBGU2GM:\n  Line 21: [![NCBI home page](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/pd-medc-pmc-cloudpmc-viewer/production/9b1d9d87/var/data/static/img/ncbi-logos/nih-nlm-ncbi--white.svg)](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/)\n\n  Line 73: ![Royal Society of Chemistry logo](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/banners/logo-rscsd.png)\n\n  Line 81: -   [View in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=\"Digit%20Discov\"[Title%20Abbreviation])\n\n  Line 158: Thermophysical properties such as the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of organic molecules are important to the performance of liquid formulations such as lubricants and refrigerants.[1](#cit1) Accurate prediction of these properties for pure organic molecules is also important for a wide range of industries, including energy, chemicals, and pharmaceuticals.[2–4](#cit2) In this context, the lack of reliable thermophysical data is one of the largest stumbling blocks in rational molecular,[5](#cit5) formulation,[6](#cit6) and process[7](#cit7) design. In particular, lubricants are required to adhere to strict viscosity limits across a wide temperature range (40–100 °C);[8](#cit8) however, for the organic molecules relevant to this application (C10–C50),[9](#cit9) viscosity–temperature data are still lacking. Viscosity data are available for many normal alkanes up to ∼C24, but data for branched alkanes, cycloalkanes, aromatics, alkyl cycloalkanes, and alkyl aromatics is much more sparse.[10](#cit10) There is also a lack of reliable thermophysical data available for oxygen-containing molecules such as esters, ethers, and glycols in the C10–C50 range.[11](#cit11) It is often possible to interpolate between missing entries with calculated predictions using a reliable equation of state, such as the perturbed-chain statistical associating fluid theory (PC-SAFT).[12,13](#cit12) Several other predictive models are available for pure hydrocarbons,[14](#cit14) with the most promising being group contribution and particularly entropy scaling methods.[15,16](#cit15) Quantitative structure–property relationship (QSPR) models[17–21](#cit17) have also been identified that are capable of predicting the viscosity of organic liquids. Within the chemical families and conditions for which they are parameterized, these methods can be highly accurate; their main limitation is reduced transferability, with predictive performance degrading outside the molecules and conditions fo...\n  Line 170: ## Methodology\n\n  Line 174: To construct a robust and diverse dataset for model development and evaluation, we collected reference data for density, thermal conductivity and dynamic viscosity at atmospheric pressure and at both 40 °C and 100 °C. Only molecules with a dynamic viscosity of approximately 1–50 mPa s at 40 °C were included, because this is the range accessible to equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations.[39](#cit39) Data were compiled from the Landolt–Börnstein series[73](#cit73) and the NIST Chemistry WebBook[74](#cit74) (accessed June 2024), resulting in 835 unique molecules carrying approximately 4700 critically evaluated reference values after the structure canonicalization, unit conversion and deduplication steps described below.\n\n  Line 178: For interpolation of the liquid density values, the NIST WebBook uses the Tait equation.[77](#cit77) For liquid thermal conductivity, the TransportPolynomial model was applied, utilizing temperature-dependent polynomial fits to experimental thermal conductivity data. For the Landolt–Börnstein series,[73](#cit73) cubic spline interpolation using a minimum of three surrounding data points was employed to estimate the property values at the desired temperatures. In cases where only the kinematic viscosity was reported, it was converted to dynamic viscosity using *µ* = *ν* × *ρ*, where *µ* is dynamic viscosity, *ν* is kinematic viscosity, and *ρ* is density. Molecules whose viscosity was reported only as kinematic and for which no density was available at the same temperature were excluded (283 molecules at each temperature). Canonicalization and deduplication of the 1187 source entries give 1114 unique molecules, of which 835 retain at least one usable label and form the dataset used here. The viscosity values were normalized by taking their natural logarithm to improve regression performance during ML training.[54](#cit54)\n\n  Line 258: Dimensionality-reduced embeddings provide a compact alternative to high-dimensional molecular descriptors by reducing noise and redundancy while retaining the most informative features.[81](#cit81) In this study, embeddings generated using principal component analysis (PCA) and uniform manifold approximation and projection (UMAP) were evaluated across a range of dimensionalities to assess their impact on model performance, as both the choice of method and retained dimensionality can influence predictive accuracy. PCA and UMAP embeddings were used as inputs to ML models to compare linear and nonlinear dimensionality reduction strategies for molecular property prediction. A detailed analysis of embedding method and dimensionality effects, including root-mean-square error (RMSE) trends across properties and model types, is provided in the SI. Embeddings were generated using the scikit-learn and UMAP-learn libraries from *z*\\-score normalized molecular descriptors.\n\n  Line 270: All models were evaluated under a single shared nested cross-validation protocol, applied to molecular representation configurations that were fixed in advance. The embedding dimensionality and the fingerprint radius and bit size were chosen by grid search on a single 80 : 10 : 10 train/validation/test split, as reported in the SI, so the protocol is nested with respect to hyperparameter selection and conditional on those representation configurations. The dataset was partitioned into five fixed outer folds, so that both temperatures of a given molecule always fall in the same fold and no molecule appears in more than one outer test set. The folds were constructed by stratified sampling over quintiles of the log-transformed 40 °C viscosity labels (molecules without a viscosity label were assigned *via* median-filled strata), then frozen. For each outer fold, hyperparameters were selected by random search over 25 configurations sampled from model-specific ranges, scored on a designated validation partition of the non-test data (approximately 85 : 15 train/validation); the selected configuration was then retrained on all non-test data and evaluated once on the untouched outer test fold. Full details of the architectures, hyperparameter ranges, and training procedures for each model are provided in the SI.\n\n  Line 284: The protocol suggested by Schmitt *et al.*[26](#cit26) was adopted in the EMD simulations because it has been shown to enable accurate calculation of the viscosity of lubricant-sized molecules. A velocity–Verlet integrator with a 1.0 fs timestep was employed.[109](#cit109) Bonds containing hydrogen atoms were constrained with the SHAKE algorithm.[110](#cit110) Constraining C–H bonds is important for obtaining accurate thermal conductivities with all-atom force fields, as it suppresses the classically over-excited high-frequency C–H stretching modes that otherwise inflate the Green–Kubo heat flux.[111](#cit111) Lennard-Jones interactions were truncated at 12 Å with long-range tail corrections applied to energy and pressure, and long-range electrostatics were treated using the particle–particle particle–mesh (PPPM) method[112](#cit112) with a relative accuracy of 10−5. Geometric mixing rules were used for the unlike-atom interactions, and intramolecular 1–4 interactions were scaled by 0.5 following the OPLS convention.\n\n  Line 332: XGBoost and RF models, when used with molecular descriptors, performed most consistently across all three transport properties. XGBoost with descriptors gave the lowest viscosity error of any combination (RMSE = 0.35 and 0.30 in ln(*η*/mPa s) at 40 and 100 °C, *R*2 = 0.86 and 0.83) and was among the best two or three combinations for density and thermal conductivity, while RF with descriptors gave the lowest density error (RMSE = 0.047 g cm−3). An error of 0.35 on the logarithmic scale corresponds to a typical discrepancy of a factor of 1.4 between predicted and measured viscosity. This aligns with previous studies,[124,125](#cit124) which also found tree-based models to be reliable across datasets. These methods handle nonlinear relationships and high-dimensional features effectively, making them suitable for the diverse information captured by molecular descriptors, and are relatively insensitive to feature scale, irregular target functions and uninformative features.[126](#cit126)\n\n  Line 452: E.O. thanks the Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (EPSRC) for PhD funding through their Doctoral Training Partnership (DTP) scheme (EP/T51780X/1). D.D. thanks the Royal Academy of Engineering (RAEng) for support through the Shell/RAEng Research Chair in Complex Engineering Interfaces. J.P.E. thanks the RAEng for support through their Research Fellowships scheme. We also acknowledge support from the Shell University Technology Centre (UTC) for Mobility and Lubricants and the EPSRC InFUSE Prosperity Partnership (EP/V038044/1). We acknowledge the use of Imperial College London Research Computing Service ([https://doi.org/10.14469/hpc/2232](https://doi.org/10.14469/hpc/2232)) and the UK Materials and Molecular Modelling Hub, which is partially funded by the EPSRC (EP/T022213/1, EP/W032260/1 and EP/P020194/1). We thank Stefan J. Eder (TU Wien) for useful discussions.\n\n  Line 488: 27.  Moore J. D. Cui S. T. Cochran H. D. Cummings P. T. Rheology of lubricant basestocks: A molecular dynamics study of C30 isomers. J. Chem. Phys. 2000;113:8833–8840. doi: 10.1063/1.1318768. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1318768)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=J.%20Chem.%20Phys.&title=Rheology%20of%20lubricant%20basestocks:%20A%20molecular%20dynamics%20study%20of%20C30%20isomers&author=J.%20D.%20Moore&author=S.%20T.%20Cui&author=H.%20D.%20Cochran&author=P.%20T.%20Cummings&volume=113&publication_year=2000&pages=8833-8840&doi=10.1063/1.1318768&)\\]\n\n  Line 545: 84.  Weininger D. SMILES, a Chemical Language and Information System: 1: Introduction to Methodology and Encoding Rules. J. Chem. Inf. Comput. Sci. 1988;28:31–36. doi: 10.1021/ci00057a005. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1021/ci00057a005)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=J.%20Chem.%20Inf.%20Comput.%20Sci.&title=SMILES,%20a%20Chemical%20Language%20and%20Information%20System:%201:%20Introduction%20to%20Methodology%20and%20Encoding%20Rules&author=D.%20Weininger&volume=28&publication_year=1988&pages=31-36&doi=10.1021/ci00057a005&)\\]\n\n  Line 583: 122.  Dysthe D. K. Fuchs A. H. Rousseau B. Fluid transport properties by equilibrium molecular dynamics. I. Methodology at extreme fluid states. J. Chem. Phys. 1999;110:4047–4059. doi: 10.1063/1.478286. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.478286)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=J.%20Chem.%20Phys.&title=Fluid%20transport%20properties%20by%20equilibrium%20molecular%20dynamics.%20I.%20Methodology%20at%20extreme%20fluid%20states&author=D.%20K.%20Dysthe&author=A.%20H.%20Fuchs&author=B.%20Rousseau&volume=110&publication_year=1999&pages=4047-4059&doi=10.1063/1.478286&)\\]\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b8f9415001OxugAIIL4cotAW:\n  Line 17:  [![arXiv logo](/static/base/1.0.1/images/arxiv-logo-primary-light.svg) Back to arXiv](/)\n\n  Line 58: Neurosymbolic systems rely on mathematical solvers to guarantee reasoning correctness, yet solvers are fundamentally blind to whether a formal translation maintains strict reference-equivalence to a designated formalization. We formalize this vulnerability as Verdict-Preserving-Unfaithfulness (VPU): a failure mode where an incorrect encoding executes successfully and matches the expected verdict. We theoretically prove that structural, verdict-only verification heuristics are mathematically bounded to chance-level detection on these deceptively valid traces. To resolve this, we introduce Generative Verification (GenV), which distills an offline Z3-equivalence oracle into a reference-free, continuous reference-equivalence score by repurposing the language model’s native vocabulary space. Mechanistic analysis via decision-projected logit lenses and sparse autoencoders shows this generative readout natively extracts precise spatial error coordinates without explicit localization training. Empirically, our oracle-mined verifier (GenV+HN) achieves 0.961 AUROC in reference-equivalence verification, generalizes zero-shot across unseen translators and divergent formal styles, and yields an 11.3-point downstream accuracy gain in agentic test-time compute allocation.\n\n  Line 64: Figure 1: Identical solver verdicts do not imply equivalent formalizations. Reversing (\\>\\>) to (<<) creates a valid, satisfiable candidate that is not logically equivalent to the reference. While a shared sat verdict cannot distinguish the pair, GenV+HN successfully separates them using only the source problem and candidate encoding.\n\n  Line 66: Neurosymbolic systems promise a clean division of effort, a language model translates a natural-language problem into formal logic, and a sound solver such as Z3 ([de Moura and Bjørner, 2008](#bib.bib2)) performs the deduction. This architecture underlies satisfiability-aided language models ([Ye et al., 2023](#bib.bib12)), logical question answering ([Pan et al., 2023](#bib.bib14); [Olausson et al., 2023](#bib.bib13)), autoformalization ([Wu et al., 2022](#bib.bib11); [Ganguly et al., 2024](#bib.bib32); [Ganguly et al., 2025](#bib.bib33)), and deployed policy checks ([Amazon Web Services, 2025](#bib.bib16); [Bayless et al., 2025](#bib.bib31)). The resulting guarantee is powerful but conditional. A sound solver certifies what follows from the supplied encoding, it does not certify that the encoding accurately represents the source problem. The translation step is therefore an independent point of failure. An encoding may parse, execute and receive a valid solver certificate while changing a comparison operator, ommiting a requirement, reverse implication or binding the wrong variable. Figure [1](#S1.F1 \"Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\") illustrates a minimal example: two encodings return the same satisfiability verdict even though they are not logically equivalent. The solver is correct in both cases. The failure lies in the correspondence between the source problem and the formal program. We study this failure relative to a designated reference formalization s⋆s^{\\\\star}. A candidate is Verdict-Preserving-Unfaithfulness (VPU) when it is syntactically valid, returns the same solver verdit as s⋆s^{\\\\star} and is not logically equivalent to s⋆s^{\\\\star}. This is an operational, reference-relative definition. It gives a deterministic target for training and evaluation, but it does not assume that every natural language problem has a unique formal expression or a reference fully captures subjective sourc...\n  Line 72: Our central design choice separates strict oracle supervision from test-time deployment. Bidirectional Z3 checks generate decisive reference-equivalence labels. At deployment, where the designated reference is absent, GenV distills this offline signal into a functional, reference-free score. GenV repurposes the model’s standard token-prediction capabilities to produce a holistic Yes/No judgment, using normalized token probability as a continuous equivalence score instead of appending specialized classification layers or aggregating local step scores. Concentrating this supervision on oracle-certified VPUs through targeted mining yields the deployed GenV+HN verifier.\n\n  Line 103: Reference Faithfulness. Let xx be a natural-language problem, ss a candidate SMT-LIBv2 encoding ([Barrett et al., 2017](#bib.bib1)), and s⋆s^{\\\\star} the designated reference encoding for xx. Let A⁡(s)A(s) denote the logical conjunction of assertions in ss, and let v⁡(s)∈{sat,unsat}v(s)\\\\in\\\\{\\\\texttt{sat},\\\\texttt{unsat}\\\\} represent its solver execution outcome. For candidates that share a compatible logical signature with the reference, we define reference-equivalence by mutual implication:\n\n  Line 121: The first condition isolates candidates for which a single solver-verdict check provides no warning. The second condition identifies a logical mismatch with the designated reference. VPU is therefore an operational category of reference mismatch.\n\n  Line 147: To address the deployment challenge defined in Equation [3](#S3.E3 \"In 3 Problem Formulation ‣ Beyond Solver Verdicts: Generative Reward Models for Autoformalization\"), we distill the offline Z3 oracle into a deployable, reference-free generative verifier. Rather than altering the underlying model architecture, our framework operates entirely within the language model’s native vocabulary space. Our methodology comprises three steps: formulating a continuous verification score via next-token prediction, executing targeted supervised fine-tuning, and isolating the logical boundary via oracle guided hard negative mining.\n\n\n(Results truncated. Consider using a more specific path or pattern.)"
