n try to fetch the grainger notes as HTML or find the raw LaTeX/slides.\n\nLet me try webfetching the grainger notes with a text extraction that might work better, or search for the specific content. Alternatively, I can find another open source with the exact \"O(log N)\" statement.\n\nActually there's a well-known open source: the NVIDIA GPU Gems / scan primitive papers. Sengupta, Harris, Zhang, Owens \"Scan primitives for GPU computing\" (2007) states scan is O(log n). But that's for scan/prefix sum (bidiagonal), not tridiagonal.\n\nFor tridiagonal cyclic reduction specifically, the grainger notes are the canonical citable source. Let me try harder to extract text. Let me try fetching with pdftotext-like behavior. The webfetch already produced raw PDF. Maybe I can find the actual slide content by reading the saved file more carefully — the text streams are FlateDecode compressed, so grep on the raw file won't find plaintext.\n\nAlternative: search for a mirror or the actual course notes in text form. Let me search \"cs554 cyclic reduction O(log n) tridiagonal\" or find the grainger notes in HTML.\n\nActually, let me step back. I have enough sources. The key facts and their citations:\n\n1. PCR/cyclic reduction solves tridiagonal in O(log N) parallel steps (vs O(N) sequential Thomas). \n   - Source: grainger notes PDF (option's source). I'll try to get exact text; if not, I'll cite the Zhang paper's abstract which says \"PCR and RD have fewer algorithmic steps but do more work each step\" and CR \"linear algorithm complexity but suffers from more algorithmic steps\". Combined with Thomas O(n) from Wikipedia. Actually \"fewer algorithmic steps\" implies O(log N) vs O(N).\n   - Actually the scienceDirect \"Thomas Algorithm - an overview\" page (open) has: \"We first characterize the complexity of three tridiagonal algorithms in terms of algorithmic steps and work per step as shown in Table 11.1. The Thomas algorithm is essentially sequential Gaussian elimination, which requires the most algorithmic steps, but the least amount of work per step.\" This is a clean citable sentence.\n\n2. Thomas algorithm O(n) operations: Wikipedia \"For such systems, the solution can be obtained in O(n) operations\".\n\n3. PCR on GPU does more work per step, memory-bound, hybrid best: Zhang et al. 2010.\n\n4. A100 specs: 108 SMs, 6912 cores, 1555 GB/s: NVIDIA whitepaper.\n\n5. cuSPARSE provides tridiagonal solver (gtsv): NVIDIA docs. Let me get that.\n\n6. PfSolve 2025 ACM paper (open? dl.acm.org full HTML) — \"High Performance Solution of Tridiagonal Systems on the GPU\" — this could be a stronger closest_prior (modern GPUs). Let me check if it's open. The ACM full HTML link dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3716171 — might be open access. Let me try fetching it. Actually it's 2025, recent, and might state PCR specifics on modern GPUs.\n\nLet me prioritize. I need:\n- cuSPARSE gtsv docs (for independent code / anchor).\n- Exact sentence from grainger notes (try again or substitute).\n- Maybe PfSolve for closest prior (or use Zhang 2010).\n\nFor the anchor: the plan checks PCR correctness against an independent solver. Options: cuSPARSE gtsv (NVIDIA library), or numpy/scipy solve_banded on CPU. scipy.linalg.solve_banded is a clean independent reference (open docs). And cuSPARSE gtsv as GPU reference. I'll cite scipy docs or cuSPARSE docs.\n\nLet me fetch cuSPARSE docs and the grainger notes text. Let me also try to get the grainger notes as text via a search for its content.\n\nActually, let me try a different extraction: use webfetch on the grainger PDF with format \"html\"? Probably still binary. Let me instead search for the exact phrase in the notes.\n\nLet me do these in parallel:\n1. webfetch cuSPARSE gtsv docs (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cusparse/index.html or a more specific URL). Let me search for the exact URL.\n2. search for grainger cs554 tridiagonal cyclic reduction \"O(log\" to find a text version.\n3. Fetch the Zhang PDF grep for exact