    "content": "Found 100 matches (more matches available)\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14c8a97a8001TiOs3p25aD48Si:\n  Line 11: [![Advertisement](//pubads.g.doubleclick.net/gampad/ad?iu=/285/npjcompumats.nature.com/article&sz=728x90&c=-904459426&t=pos%3Dtop%26type%3Darticle%26artid%3Ds41524-025-01720-4%26doi%3D10.1038/s41524-025-01720-4%26subjmeta%3D1034,1037,301,403,563,638,639%26kwrd%3DOrganic+chemistry,Computational+methods,Theoretical+chemistry)](//pubads.g.doubleclick.net/gampad/jump?iu=/285/npjcompumats.nature.com/article&sz=728x90&c=-904459426&t=pos%3Dtop%26type%3Darticle%26artid%3Ds41524-025-01720-4%26doi%3D10.1038/s41524-025-01720-4%26subjmeta%3D1034,1037,301,403,563,638,639%26kwrd%3DOrganic+chemistry,Computational+methods,Theoretical+chemistry)\n\n  Line 72: The escalating energy crisis has spurred extensive research into organic compounds for energy-efficient applications, taking advantage of their environmental friendliness, cost-effective synthesis, and adaptable molecular structures. Traditional trial-and-error methods for discovering highly functional organic compounds are expensive and time-consuming. We employed a 3D transformer-based molecular representation learning algorithm to create the Org-Mol pre-trained model, using 60 million semi-empirically optimized small organic molecule structures. After fine-tuning with public experimental data, the model can accurately predict various physical properties of pure organics, with test set *R*2 values exceeding 0.92. These fine-tuned models are used in high-throughput screening among millions of ester molecules to identify novel immersion coolants, resulting in the experimental validation of two promising candidates. This work not only demonstrates the potential of Org-Mol in predicting bulk properties for pure organic compounds but also paves the way for the rational and efficient development of ideal candidates for energy-saving materials.\n\n  Line 78: ### [Efficient screening framework for organic solar cells with deep learning and ensemble learning](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41524-023-01155-9?fromPaywallRec=false)\n\n  Line 105: Amidst the current energy crisis, the research and development of materials that are energy-efficient and/or offer high energy utilization efficiency have become an immediate imperative and a focal point in scientific inquiry[1](#ref-CR1 \"Mirkin, C. A., Sargent, E. H. & Schrag, D. P. Energy transition needs new materials. Science 384, 713–713 (2024).\"),[2](#ref-CR2 \"Davidson, D. J. Exnovating for a renewable energy transition. Nat. Energy 4, 254–256 (2019).\"),[3](#ref-CR3 \"Zhang, Q., Sun, Y., Xu, W. & Zhu, D. Organic thermoelectric materials: Emerging green energy materials converting heat to electricity directly and efficiently. Adv. Mater. 26, 6829–6851 (2014).\"),[4](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR4 \"Kandpal, V., Jaswal, A., Santibanez Gonzalez, E. D. R. & Agarwal, N. Energy Efficiency and Renewable Energy Technologies, 89–123 \n\n  Line 107: (Springer Nature Switzerland, Cham, 2024).\"). Organic compounds play a pivotal role in this context, owing to their environmental friendliness, low-cost processing, and almost infinite modifiability to meet a wide array of property requirements. Prospective applications lie in the fields such as immersion coolants for data centers[5](#ref-CR5 \"Kuncoro, I. W. et al. Immersion cooling as the next technology for data center cooling: A review. In Journal of Physics: Conference Series, vol. 1402, 044057 (IOP Publishing, 2019).\"),[6](#ref-CR6 \"Kanbur, B. B., Wu, C., Fan, S. & Duan, F. System-level experimental investigations of the direct immersion cooling data center units with thermodynamic and thermoeconomic assessments. Energy 217, 119373 (2021).\"),[7](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR7 \"Huang, Y. et al. Experimental study on the immersion liquid cooling performance of high-power data center servers. Energy 297, 131195 (2024).\"), phase change materials for thermal energy storage[8](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR8 \"Sharma, R., Ganesan, P., Tyagi, V., Metselaar, H. & Sandaran, S. Developments in organic solid–liquid phase change materials and their applications in thermal energy storage. Energy Convers. Manag. 95, 193–228 (2015).\"),[9](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR9 \"Li, B. et al. Colossal barocaloric effects in plastic crystals. Nature 567, 506–510 (2019).\"), and liquid organic hydrogen carriers[10](#ref-CR10 \"Preuster, P., Papp, C. & Wasserscheid, P. Liquid organic hydrogen carriers (LOHCS): Toward a hydrogen-free hydrogen economy. Acc. Chem. Res. 50, 74–85 (2017).\"),[11](#ref-CR11 \"Mah, A. X. Y. et al. Targeting and scheduling of standalone renewable energy system with liquid organic hydrogen carrier as energy storage. Energy 218, 119475 (2021).\"),[12](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR12 \"Tan, R., Ji, Q., Ling, Y. & Li, L. Advances in liquid organic hydrogen carriers: Developing efficient dehydrogenation strategies. Chem. Commun. 60, 8186–8203 (2024).\"...\n  Line 109: Although the measurement of the properties mentioned above for a single compound is straightforward[15](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR15 \"Nadkarni, R. & Nadkarni, R. Guide to ASTM Test Methods for the Analysis of Petroleum Products and Lubricants, vol. 44 (ASTM International West Conshohocken, 2007).\"), the synthesis of various candidates can be time-consuming. Therefore, the experimental trial-and-error cost of exploring the chemical space to develop ideal energy-saving organic materials remains high. Multiple attempts have been made to predict these electrical, mechanical, and thermal properties of organic compounds via molecular dynamics (MD) and/or machine learning (ML) methods[16](#ref-CR16 \"Yamaguchi, T., Yamada, H., Fujiwara, T. & Hori, K. Simulations of dielectric constants and viscosities of organic electrolytes by quantum mechanics and molecular dynamics. J. Mol. Liq. 312, 113288 (2020).\"),[17](#ref-CR17 \"Caleman, C. et al. Force field benchmark of organic liquids: Density, enthalpy of vaporization, heat capacities, surface tension, isothermal compressibility, volumetric expansion coefficient, and dielectric constant. J. Chem. Theory Comput. 8, 61–74 (2012).\"),[18](#ref-CR18 \"Ju, S.-P., Huang, S.-C., Lin, K.-H., Chen, H.-Y. & Shen, T.-K. Prediction of optical and dielectric properties of 4-cyano-4-pentylbiphenyl liquid crystals by molecular dynamics simulation, coarse-grained dynamics simulation, and density functional theory calculation. J. Phys. Chem. C. 120, 14277–14288 (2016).\"),[19](#ref-CR19 \"Sild, S. & Karelson, M. A general qspr treatment for dielectric constants of organic compounds. J. Chem. Inf. Comput. Sci. 42, 360–367 (2002).\"),[20](#ref-CR20 \"Meng, X., Jia, M. & Wang, T. Neural network prediction of biodiesel kinematic viscosity at 313 k. Fuel 121, 133–140 (2014).\"),[21](#ref-CR21 \"Deng, J. & Jia, G. Dielectric constant prediction of pure organic liquids and their mixtures with water based on interpretable machine learning. Fluid Phase E...\n  Line 111: With the development of deep learning algorithms, highly efficient screening and rational design of novel materials with required properties have been further made simple[30](#ref-CR30 \"Walters, W. P. & Barzilay, R. Applications of deep learning in molecule generation and molecular property prediction. Acc. Chem. Res. 54, 263–270 (2020).\"),[31](#ref-CR31 \"Meftahi, N. et al. Machine learning property prediction for organic photovoltaic devices. npj Comput. Mater. 6, 166 (2020).\"),[32](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR32 \"Pang, C., Tong, H. H. & Wei, L. Advanced deep learning methods for molecular property prediction. Quant. Biol. 11, 395–404 (2023).\"). For instance, Uni-Mol, a 3D transformer-based molecular representation learning (MRL) algorithm[33](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR33 \"Zhou, G. et al. Uni-mol: A universal 3d molecular representation learning framework. In The Eleventh International Conference on Learning Representations\n\n  Line 113: (2023).\"), has been successfully applied in the prediction of photophysical properties of organic photoluminescence systems[34](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR34 \"Cheng, Z. et al. Automatic screen-out of ir (iii) complex emitters by combined machine learning and computational analysis. Adv. Opt. Mater. 11, 2301093 (2023).\") and the adsorption energies of various gaseous molecules on metal-organic-frameworks[35](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR35 \"Wang, J. et al. A comprehensive transformer-based approach for high-accuracy gas adsorption predictions in metal-organic frameworks. Nat. Commun. 15, 1904 (2024).\"). One of the most favored advantages of Uni-Mol is that sufficient and rational descriptors can be automatically constructed via the pre-training process, requiring only a single molecular structures as input, and the pre-trained model can be further optimized by the subsequent fine-tuning with a small number of labeled data[33](#ref-CR33 \"Zhou, G. et al. Uni-mol: A universal 3d molecular representation learning framework. In The Eleventh International Conference on Learning Representations\n\n  Line 115: (2023).\"),[34](#ref-CR34 \"Cheng, Z. et al. Automatic screen-out of ir (iii) complex emitters by combined machine learning and computational analysis. Adv. Opt. Mater. 11, 2301093 (2023).\"),[35](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR35 \"Wang, J. et al. A comprehensive transformer-based approach for high-accuracy gas adsorption predictions in metal-organic frameworks. Nat. Commun. 15, 1904 (2024).\"). While most of the previously reported properties predicted by the Uni-Mol framework depend solely on a single molecular structure or on a single cell of a periodic crystallized structure, its feasibility in predicting bulk properties for amorphous (or liquid phase) organic compounds is yet to be validated. If proven effective, such a protocol may remarkably streamline the model-constructing process and reduce the computational cost in predicting a variety of physical properties for pure amorphous organics.\n\n  Line 117: Keeping these in mind and to facilitate the discovery of energy-saving materials in terms of pure organics, we apply the Uni-Mol framework to develop a pre-trained model specifically designed for organic compounds, namely, the Org-Mol model, followed by the fine-tuning process over various physical properties with public experimental data. Our results show that even though only the single molecular coordinates are provided during the pre-training process, the fine-tuned models for all bulk properties of pure organic small molecules achieve significantly high accuracy (with the *R*2 value of the test set higher than 0.95). As a proof-of-concept, we also assess how effectively Org-Mol can fine-tune the glass transition temperature of homopolymers using the structure of the repeating unit as input, achieving an *R*2 score above 0.92 on the test set. Notably, these fine-tuned models are applied in a high-throughput screening for the discovery of novel immersion coolants over millions of ester molecules. Hundreds of candidates are finally filtered out, and two of them are experimentally synthesized and validated, highlighting the capability of Org-Mol to directly bridge data to discovery. Notwithstanding that the current study focuses on single-component organic systems, the prediction framework established here can be readily adapted to other energy-saving materials (e.g., phase-change materials) via the same fine-tuning process.\n\n  Line 173: For thermal properties, we first take the specific heat at constant pressure *C**p* (or the heat capacity), and the thermal conductivity *κ* as two representatives to examine the performance of Org-Mol. As shown in Fig. [4](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig4)a–d, the correlation between the Org-Mol predicted values and the experimental counterparts for both of these two thermal properties remains high. The *R*2 values for the training and test sets are 0.993 and 0.982 for the heat capacity, respectively, with very small MAEs, evincing the capability of Org-Mol in the accurate prediction of thermal properties. Note that even though only 252 data points are collected for thermal conductivity, the performance of Org-Mol on the test set is still satisfactory (with the *R*2 values 0.958 for the test set), demonstrating its strength of tackling data with relatively small sample size. Furthermore, we assess the ability of Org-Mol in predicting properties of polymer systems by taking the glass transition temperature (*T**g*) as an example. We fine-tune Org-Mol on *T**g* data for around 300 homopolymers using repeating-unit structures as input (capping by hydrogen). As shown in Fig. [4](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig4)e and f, the model achieves an *R*2 of 0.926 and MAE of 25.4K on the test set, outperforming previously reported MD-based predictions[43](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR43 \"Afzal, M. A. F. et al. High-throughput molecular dynamics simulations and validation of thermophysical properties of polymers for various applications. ACS Appl. Polym. Mater. 3, 620–630 (2021).\") and demonstrating the potential generalizability of Org-Mol to homopolymer systems. It should be noted that predicting complex polymer properties remains challenging, especially for more sophisticated properties and those of copolymers, and specialized models like MMPolymer[44](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR44 \"Wang, F. et al. Mmpolymer: A multimodal multitask pretraining framework for po...\n  Line 187: To validate the accuracy of Org-Mol across the aforementioned properties of organic small molecules, we conduct comparative experiments using three state-of-the-art methods: two graph neural networks (EGNN[46](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR46 \"Satorras, V. G., Hoogeboom, E. & Welling, M. E (n) equivariant graph neural networks. In International conference on machine learning, 9323–9332 (PMLR, 2021).\") and NequIP[47](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR47 \"Batzner, S. et al. E (3)-equivariant graph neural networks for data-efficient and accurate interatomic potentials. Nat. Commun. 13, 2453 (2022).\")), and a tree-based algorithm (XGBoost[48](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR48 \"Chen, T. & Guestrin, C. Xgboost: A scalable tree boosting system. In Proceedings of the 22nd ACM SIGKDD International Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining, KDD ’16, 785-794 \n\n  Line 189: (Association for Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA, 2016).\")) with Morgan fingerprints as molecular descriptors. Hyperparameters are optimized for each model to ensure fair comparison. The metrics for the test set are summarized in Table [2](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Tab2). While graph neural network algorithms (EGNN and NequIP) outperform the tree-based model XGBoost, Org-Mol demonstrates superior performance across all evaluated properties. Notably, substantial enhancements are observed for dielectric constant and thermal conductivity, two properties inherently challenging due to their complexity and sparse experimental data, underscoring the efficacy of Org-Mol’s pre-trained architecture in handling complex and/or data-scarce scenarios.\n\n  Line 197: **Table 3 Comparison of previously reported models and this work for the heat capacity *C**p***\n\n  Line 215: Monoesters with dielectric constants lower than 3.20 are then fed to the kinetic viscosity model and then the thermal conductivity model. Viscosity is prioritized over thermal conductivity due to its significant impact on system efficiency and operational feasibility. Based on the aforementioned criteria of the kinematic viscosity and thermal conductivity, 461 monoesters are finally filtered out. By further incorporating manual synthetic feasibility assessments based on experimental expertise, we select two candidates for synthesis, and their physical properties are experimentally measured. The whole sequential screening process is summarized in Scheme [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Sch1) with the molecular structures of two experimentally synthesized candidates. Detailed synthesis method and characterization analysis are given in the [Supporting Information](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#MOESM1). As shown in Table [4](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Tab4), the predicted values for all tested properties of these two candidates are in good agreement with the experimental results. A slight systematic overestimation is observed for both the kinematic viscosity and the thermal conductivity, presumably due to the inconsistency of the measuring methods between the current work and the referenced works contributing to the data set. (See [Supporting Information](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#MOESM1) for testing methods applied in this work.) It should be noticed that even though the thermal conductivity of the second candidates is slightly lower than the preferred value (0.140 W/m ⋅ K), its low dielectric constant ensures superior electrical insulation, which is particularly crucial for practical applications. Overall, the Org-Mol fine-tuned models succeed in screening out promising candidates for immersion coolants over millions of candidates, and can be further applied in the discovery of other energy-saving materials.\n\n  Line 223: Schematic graph of the screening procedure and molecular structures of two filtered-out esters as promising immersion cooling candidates. Over 6 million esters are initially constructed from saturated carboxylic acid and alcohol and are fed into the sequential screening process. Upon further consideration of manual synthetic feasibility, two candidates, Ester 1 and Ester 2, are chosen for synthesis.\n\n  Line 231: In conclusion, we have applied the framework of Uni-Mol, a 3D MRL algorithm, and developed the Org-Mol pre-trained model, which is specifically designed for organic compounds on a vast dataset of 60 million semi-empirically optimized structures. Leveraging the power of 3D transformer-based algorithms, we have successfully fine-tuned the Org-Mol pre-trained model, achieving *R*2 values exceeding 0.95 for test sets across various physical properties of pure organic small molecules. Furthermore, Org-Mol’s ability to predict glass transition temperatures of homopolymers using repeating-unit structures (test set *R*2 > 0.92) highlights its potential for polymeric systems. This high level of accuracy not only surpasses current state-of-the-art models but also offers a practical solution to the challenges posed by traditional experimental methods, which are often costly and time-consuming.\n\n  Line 235: To summarize, the Org-Mol model provides an important advancement in predicting properties of pure organic compounds, with far-reaching implications for energy-saving applications. Although the scope of the current work is limited to single-component systems, the demonstrated accuracy in predicting electrical, mechanical, and thermal properties-including its generalization to homopolymer glass transition temperatures—offers a foundational framework for rational design of tailored amorphous materials, as evinced by the experimental validation of immersion cooling candidates for energy-efficient electronics. While challenges in complex assemblies (e.g., mixtures, copolymers) necessitate further innovation, the model’s modularity and performance in pure organics paves the way for guiding the design of novel energy-efficient materials.\n\n  Line 241: We apply the framework of Uni-Mol to construct a pre-trained model, particularly designed for organic molecules containing C, H, O, N, S, Se, B, and halogens, namely the Org-Mol pre-trained model. This pre-trained model is constructed based on the PubChemQC PM6 data sets[51](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR51 \"Nakata, M., Shimazaki, T., Hashimoto, M. & Maeda, T. Pubchemqc pm6: Data sets of 221 million molecules with optimized molecular geometries and electronic properties. J. Chem. Inf. Model. 60, 5891–5899 (2020).\"). (See ref. [33](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR33 \"Zhou, G. et al. Uni-mol: A universal 3d molecular representation learning framework. In The Eleventh International Conference on Learning Representations\n\n  Line 243: (2023).\") for the detailed architecture of the pre-training process.) Specifically, PM6[52](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR52 \"Stewart, J. J. Optimization of parameters for semiempirical methods v: Modification of nddo approximations and application to 70 elements. J. Mol. Model. 13, 1173–1213 (2007).\")\\-optimized structures of 60 million small organic molecules containing the aforementioned elements are fed into the pre-training framework to initiate the self-supervised task including the 3D coordinates recovery and masked atom prediction. The Org-Mol pre-trained model is then fine-tuned via multiple experimentally measured physical properties of general single-component organic compounds, i.e., the dielectric constant near 25 °C collected from ref. [53](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR53 \"Haynes, W. CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics. CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics \n\n  Line 283: Open-source code: The Uni-Mol framework (used as the basis for Org-Mol) is freely available on GitHub ([https://github.com/DeepModeling/Uni-Mol](https://github.com/DeepModeling/Uni-Mol)).\n\n  Line 299: 4.  Kandpal, V., Jaswal, A., Santibanez Gonzalez, E. D. R. & Agarwal, N. *Energy Efficiency and Renewable Energy Technologies*, 89–123 [https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-52943-6\\_3](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-52943-6_3) (Springer Nature Switzerland, Cham, 2024).\n\n  Line 301: 5.  Kuncoro, I. W. et al. Immersion cooling as the next technology for data center cooling: A review. In *Journal of Physics: Conference Series*, vol. 1402, 044057 (IOP Publishing, 2019).\n\n  Line 357: 20.  Meng, X., Jia, M. & Wang, T. Neural network prediction of biodiesel kinematic viscosity at 313 k. *Fuel* **121**, 133–140 (2014).\n\n  Line 359:     [Article](https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.fuel.2013.12.029)  [CAS](/articles/cas-redirect/1:CAS:528:DC%2BC2cXhvVaisLk%3D)  [Google Scholar](http://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?&title=Neural%20network%20prediction%20of%20biodiesel%20kinematic%20viscosity%20at%20313%20k&journal=Fuel&doi=10.1016%2Fj.fuel.2013.12.029&volume=121&pages=133-140&publication_year=2014&author=Meng%2CX&author=Jia%2CM&author=Wang%2CT) \n\n  Line 365: 22.  Luo, S., Deng, J. & Jia, G. Physical information neural network combined with the symbolic regression: A machine learning method for prediction of dielectric constants in organic liquids and water mixtures. *J. Mol. Liq.* **417**, 126544 (2024).\n\n  Line 379: 26.  Krishnamoorthy, A. et al. Dielectric constant of liquid water determined with neural network quantum molecular dynamics. *Phys. Rev. Lett.* **126**, 216403 (2021).\n\n  Line 381:     [Article](https://doi.org/10.1103%2FPhysRevLett.126.216403)  [CAS](/articles/cas-redirect/1:CAS:528:DC%2BB3MXhtlCjtbrJ)  [PubMed](http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=Retrieve&db=PubMed&dopt=Abstract&list_uids=34114857)  [Google Scholar](http://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?&title=Dielectric%20constant%20of%20liquid%20water%20determined%20with%20neural%20network%20quantum%20molecular%20dynamics&journal=Phys.%20Rev.%20Lett.&doi=10.1103%2FPhysRevLett.126.216403&volume=126&publication_year=2021&author=Krishnamoorthy%2CA) \n\n  Line 391: 29.  Gao, N., Wang, X., Xuan, Y. & Chen, G. An artificial neural network for the residual isobaric heat capacity of liquid hfc and hfo refrigerants. *Int. J. Refrig.* **98**, 381–387 (2019).\n\n  Line 393:     [Article](https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.ijrefrig.2018.10.016)  [CAS](/articles/cas-redirect/1:CAS:528:DC%2BC1cXit1ymsrnI)  [Google Scholar](http://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?&title=An%20artificial%20neural%20network%20for%20the%20residual%20isobaric%20heat%20capacity%20of%20liquid%20hfc%20and%20hfo%20refrigerants&journal=Int.%20J.%20Refrig.&doi=10.1016%2Fj.ijrefrig.2018.10.016&volume=98&pages=381-387&publication_year=2019&author=Gao%2CN&author=Wang%2CX&author=Xuan%2CY&author=Chen%2CG) \n\n  Line 407: 33.  Zhou, G. et al. Uni-mol: A universal 3d molecular representation learning framework. In *The Eleventh International Conference on Learning Representations*[https://openreview.net/forum?id=6K2RM6wVqKu](https://openreview.net/forum?id=6K2RM6wVqKu) (2023).\n\n  Line 413: 35.  Wang, J. et al. A comprehensive transformer-based approach for high-accuracy gas adsorption predictions in metal-organic frameworks. *Nat. Commun.* **15**, 1904 (2024).\n\n  Line 415:     [Article](https://doi.org/10.1038%2Fs41467-024-46276-x)  [CAS](/articles/cas-redirect/1:CAS:528:DC%2BB2cXlsVKnu7c%3D)  [PubMed](http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=Retrieve&db=PubMed&dopt=Abstract&list_uids=38429314)  [PubMed Central](http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10907743)  [Google Scholar](http://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?&title=A%20comprehensive%20transformer-based%20approach%20for%20high-accuracy%20gas%20adsorption%20predictions%20in%20metal-organic%20frameworks&journal=Nat.%20Commun.&doi=10.1038%2Fs41467-024-46276-x&volume=15&publication_year=2024&author=Wang%2CJ) \n\n  Line 449: 44.  Wang, F. et al. Mmpolymer: A multimodal multitask pretraining framework for polymer property prediction. In *Proceedings of the 33rd ACM International Conference on Information and Knowledge Management*, CIKM ’24, 2336-2346 [https://doi.org/10.1145/3627673.3679684](https://doi.org/10.1145/3627673.3679684) (Association for Computing Machinery, New York, NY, USA, 2024).\n\n  Line 455: 46.  Satorras, V. G., Hoogeboom, E. & Welling, M. E (n) equivariant graph neural networks. In *International conference on machine learning*, 9323–9332 (PMLR, 2021).\n\n  Line 457: 47.  Batzner, S. et al. E (3)-equivariant graph neural networks for data-efficient and accurate interatomic potentials. *Nat. Commun.* **13**, 2453 (2022).\n\n  Line 459:     [Article](https://doi.org/10.1038%2Fs41467-022-29939-5)  [CAS](/articles/cas-redirect/1:CAS:528:DC%2BB38Xht1ShtLrE)  [PubMed](http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?cmd=Retrieve&db=PubMed&dopt=Abstract&list_uids=35508450)  [PubMed Central](http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9068614)  [Google Scholar](http://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?&title=E%20%283%29-equivariant%20graph%20neural%20networks%20for%20data-efficient%20and%20accurate%20interatomic%20potentials&journal=Nat.%20Commun.&doi=10.1038%2Fs41467-022-29939-5&volume=13&publication_year=2022&author=Batzner%2CS) \n\n  Line 494: This work was supported by research grants from China Petroleum & Chemical Corp (funding number 124014). N.W. thanks the financial support from the National Key R&D Program of China (Grant No. 2024YFA1510200).\n\n  Line 509: 2.  DP Technology, Beijing, China\n\n  Line 593: [![Check for updates. Verify currency and authenticity via CrossMark](data:image/svg+xml;base64,...\n  Line 747: [![Springer Nature](/oscar-static/images/logos/sn-logo-white-c8f7a9c061.svg)](https://www.springernature.com/)\n\n  Line 753: ![Nature Briefing](/static/images/logos/nature-briefing-logo-n150-white-afc2e6ccc7.svg)\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b686363001vHsApeDEyr3MVm:\n  Line 17:  [![arXiv logo](/static/base/1.0.1/images/arxiv-logo-primary-light.svg) Back to arXiv](/)\n\n  Line 23: 3.  [2 Methodology](#S2 \"In Kimina-Prover Preview: Towards Large Formal Reasoning Models with Reinforcement Learning\")\n\n  Line 24:     1.  [2.1 Autoformalization for Constructing a Diverse Base Problem Set](#S2.SS1 \"In 2 Methodology ‣ Kimina-Prover Preview: Towards Large Formal Reasoning Models with Reinforcement Learning\")\n\n  Line 25:     2.  [2.2 Formal Reasoning Pattern](#S2.SS2 \"In 2 Methodology ‣ Kimina-Prover Preview: Towards Large Formal Reasoning Models with Reinforcement Learning\")\n\n  Line 26:     3.  [2.3 Reinforcement Learning](#S2.SS3 \"In 2 Methodology ‣ Kimina-Prover Preview: Towards Large Formal Reasoning Models with Reinforcement Learning\")\n\n  Line 62: #  ![[Uncaptioned image]](2504.11354v1/figures/logo_kn.png) Kimina-Prover Preview: Towards Large Formal Reasoning Models with Reinforcement Learning\n\n  Line 68: We introduce Kimina-Prover Preview, a large language model that pioneers a novel reasoning-driven exploration paradigm for formal theorem proving, as showcased in this preview release. Trained with a large-scale reinforcement learning pipeline from Qwen2.5-72B, Kimina-Prover demonstrates strong performance in Lean 4 proof generation by employing a structured reasoning pattern we term formal reasoning pattern. This approach allows the model to emulate human problem-solving strategies in Lean, iteratively generating and refining proof steps. Kimina-Prover sets a new state-of-the-art on the miniF2F benchmark, reaching 80.7% with pass@8192. Beyond improved benchmark performance, our work yields several key insights: (1) Kimina-Prover exhibits high sample efficiency, delivering strong results even with minimal sampling (pass@1) and scaling effectively with computational budget, stemming from its unique reasoning pattern and RL training; (2) we demonstrate clear performance scaling with model size, a trend previously unobserved for neural theorem provers in formal mathematics; (3) the learned reasoning style, distinct from traditional search algorithms, shows potential to bridge the gap between formal verification and informal mathematical intuition. We open source distilled versions with 1.5B and 7B parameters of Kimina-Prover11 1 [https://github.com/MoonshotAI/Kimina-Prover-Preview](https://github.com/MoonshotAI/Kimina-Prover-Preview).\n\n  Line 80: Recent advances in neural theorem proving have focused on leveraging large language models (LLMs) to tackle the inherent challenges of formal reasoning in proof assistants like Lean 4 ([Moura & Ullrich, 2021](#bib.bibx16)) or Isabelle ([Nipkow et al., 2002](#bib.bibx17)). Initial approaches focused on training LLMs to generate individual proof steps or tactics within interactive proof assistants ([Polu et al., 2022](#bib.bibx20); [Wu et al., 2024](#bib.bibx28); [Wang, 2023](#bib.bibx24); [Deepmind, 2024](#bib.bibx3)), often coupling the LLM’s predictive capabilities with classical search algorithms like Best-First Search (BFS) ([Polu et al., 2022](#bib.bibx20); [Wu et al., 2024](#bib.bibx28); [Xin et al., 2025](#bib.bibx30)) or Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) ([Lample et al., 2022](#bib.bibx10); [Wang, 2023](#bib.bibx24); [Deepmind, 2024](#bib.bibx3)) to explore the proof space. Other strategies involved training models to generate entire proof structures from a given state ([Xin et al., 2024](#bib.bibx29); [Lin et al., 2025a](#bib.bibx15); [First et al., 2023](#bib.bibx5)). Despite notable progress, these existing methods face significant challenges. The reliance on explicit search algorithms (BFS, MCTS) introduces substantial computational overhead and complexity, limiting scalability. Furthermore, while LLMs excel at pattern matching and sequence generation, effectively capturing the deep, structured, and often non-linear reasoning required for complex formal proofs remains difficult. Standard supervised fine-tuning or basic chain-of-thought prompting may not sufficiently elicit the sophisticated reasoning necessary. Critically, previous neural theorem provers tailored for formal mathematics have generally not demonstrated clear improvements in performance corresponding to increases in model size, suggesting limitations in their ability to leverage larger model capacity for enhanced reasoning. While some works ([Lin et al., 2025](#bib.bibx14); [Wang et al., 2025](...\n  Line 82: In this work, we introduce Kimina-Prover Preview, representing an early attempt to bridge this gap by pioneering a novel reasoning-driven exploration paradigm for formal theorem proving. Built upon the Kimi k1.5 reinforcement learning (RL) pipeline, which has demonstrated success in eliciting long chain-of-thought reasoning for complex informal math and coding tasks ([Kimi-Team, 2025](#bib.bibx9)), Kimina-Prover is specifically adapted for formal reasoning within the Lean 4 proof assistant. Instead of relying on external search algorithms, our approach leverages the LLM’s own internal reasoning capabilities, enhanced through large-scale RL with carefully designed reward signals and structured reasoning patterns. This allows the model to emulate human problem-solving strategies, implicitly exploring the proof space and iteratively generating and refining proof steps through a process guided by its internal reasoning tokens. Our contributions include:\n\n  Line 97: ## 2 Methodology\n\n  Line 111: One of the critical challenges for reasoning LLMs to excel at formal theorem proving is the lack of alignment between informal mathematical reasoning data and its translation to formal proofs. To tackle this, we design a novel formal reasoning pattern to enable Kimina-Prover to think in an environment that aligns the informal and formal mathematical reasoning. During training, we filter for responses that format their thinking in between <think> …  </think> tokens and output the final proof between chosen special tokens. Within the thinking block, we seek informal-formal alignment by interspersing informal reasoning with relevant Lean 4 code snippets, also marked by special tokens. To enforce thinking block alignment with the final proof, we ensure that the majority of the Lean 4 code snippets appear in the final proof. With this reasoning pattern (see Figure [2](#S2.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ Kimina-Prover Preview: Towards Large Formal Reasoning Models with Reinforcement Learning\") on the left), we observe scaling of output token length, which correlates with passing proof-complexity from our evaluations.\n\n  Line 115: Cold start. To kickstart the models’ ability to output formal proofs following our formal reasoning pattern, we perform a minimal supervised fine-tuning run before starting our reinforcement learning training loop. In particular, we collect a dataset of olympiad-style mathematics problems with statements and solutions both in natural language format and formalized in Lean 4. We then ask Claude 3.7 Sonnet ([Anthropic, 2025](#bib.bibx1)) to synthesize a thinking block output combining the the informal and formal proofs to synthesize a mini-SFT dataset of around 20K examples (we have tried several LLMs and only Claude’s performance is satisfying). This significantly boosts the models’ ability to align the informal and formal reasoning steps and enhances downstream performance during the RL phase.\n\n  Line 121: Following the supervised fine-tuning (SFT) phase, we employ reinforcement learning (RL) to further enhance our model’s formal theorem-proving capabilities, as illustrated in the pipeline schematic (Figure [2](#S2.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ Kimina-Prover Preview: Towards Large Formal Reasoning Models with Reinforcement Learning\")). The RL process iteratively refines the model’s policy. Each iteration begins by sampling a batch of N\\=1000N=1000 problems from our established problem set. For each problem, the current policy generates k\\=8k=8 candidate solutions (rollouts). The final Lean 4 code of each candidate is then rigorously verified using the Lean compiler to determine correctness. A binary reward signal is assigned: 11 for a completely correct proof and 00 otherwise. In line with the methodology of Kimi k1.5 ([Kimi-Team, 2025](#bib.bibx9)), we employ the following loss to optimize the language model:\n\n  Line 123: L⁡(θ)\\=𝔼(x,y∗)∼D​\\[𝔼(y,z)∼πold​\\[r⁡(x,y,y∗)−τ​log​Z−τ​log​πθ​(y,z|x)πold​(y,z|x)\\]\\],L(\\\\theta)=\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{(x,y^{\\*})\\\\sim D}\\\\left\\[\\\\mathbb{E}\\_{(y,z)\\\\sim\\\\pi\\_{\\\\text{old}}}\\\\left\\[r(x,y,y^{\\*})-\\\\tau\\\\log Z-\\\\tau\\\\log\\\\frac{\\\\pi\\_{\\\\theta}(y,z|x)}{\\\\pi\\_{\\\\text{old}}(y,z|x)}\\\\right\\]\\\\right\\]\\\\,,\n\n  Line 127: where πold\\\\pi\\_{\\\\text{old}} is the previous policy model and the normalization constant log⁡Z\\\\log Z is approximated using the empirical mean of the rewards in practice. During RL training, we see strong scaling of formal reasoning at test time. However, limited SFT data and formal structure cause early RL format collapse from negative gradients. To prevent this, we apply format filtering with two key constraints: (1) each generated sample must contain at least one tactic block; and (2) tactic blocks must collectively cover at least 60% of the Lean code included in the final Lean 4 solution. Moreover, To counter format collapse from negative gradients, we randomly discard samples with negative gradients (probability ω\\=0.5\\\\omega=0.5). Combined with mixed training on informal math data, this stabilizes training and promotes more sophisticated formal reasoning.\n\n  Line 129: In practice, our RL training is conducted starting from Qwen2.5-72B ([Qwen-Team, 2024](#bib.bibx21)). We maintain a constant learning rate of (2×10−6)(2\\\\times 10^{-6}) and a fixed KL divergence coefficient τ\\=0.4\\\\tau=0.4 (Equation [1](#S2.E1 \"In 2.3 Reinforcement Learning ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ Kimina-Prover Preview: Towards Large Formal Reasoning Models with Reinforcement Learning\")). This KL constraint ensures stability by controlling the policy’s deviation from the initial supervised policy throughout the RL process.\n\n  Line 139: Lean Server. In our reinforcement learning and evaluation pipelines, we integrate the Numina Lean Server ([Numina, 2025](#bib.bibx18)) as the verification backend to provide real-time feedback for generated proof attempts. Built upon Lean FRO’s LeanREPL ([Lean FRO, 2023](#bib.bibx11)), the Numina Lean Server employs an LRU-based caching mechanism that reuses preloaded environments based on import headers, significantly reducing initialization overhead. Furthermore, it supports extensive parallelization across multiple CPUs by managing multiple Lean REPL processes concurrently. These innovations result in a 10×10\\\\times speedup in verification throughput, achieving up to 100 iterations per second on machines equipped with 64 CPU cores and 512 GB RAM. During RL training, this verification system operates efficiently in the rollout phase, evaluating proofs in real time as they are generated. Due to the relatively slow proof-generation process, verification does not become a bottleneck, requiring only 640 CPU cores for training. This efficiency contrasts with previous language model-based theorem proving approaches, which typically require thousands of CPU cores to sustain real-time verification at scale ([Lample et al., 2022](#bib.bibx10); [Xin et al., 2024](#bib.bibx29)).\n\n  Line 358: -   Deepmind (2024) Deepmind “AI achieves silver-medal standard solving International Mathematical Olympiad problems”, 2024 URL: [https://deepmind.google/discover/blog/ai-solves-imo-problems-at-silver-medal-level/](https://deepmind.google/discover/blog/ai-solves-imo-problems-at-silver-medal-level/)\n\n  Line 368: -   Li et al. (2025) Yang Li et al. “HunyuanProver: A Scalable Data Synthesis Framework and Guided Tree Search for Automated Theorem Proving”, 2025 arXiv: [https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.20735](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.20735)\n\n  Line 372: -   Nipkow et al. (2002) Tobias Nipkow, Lawrence Paulson and Markus Wenzel “Isabelle/HOL: a proof assistant for higher-order logic” Springer Science & Business Media, 2002\n\n  Line 376: -   Qwen-Team (2024) Qwen-Team “Qwen2.5: A Party of Foundation Models”, 2024 URL: [https://qwenlm.github.io/blog/qwen2.5/](https://qwenlm.github.io/blog/qwen2.5/)\n\n  Line 377: -   Renshaw (2024) David Renshaw “Compfiles: Catalog Of Math Problems Formalized In Lean” GitHub repository, [https://github.com/dwrensha/compfiles](https://github.com/dwrensha/compfiles), 2024\n\n  Line 439: Recent approaches to automated theorem proving have combined large language models with formal proof assistants such as Lean 4. These systems typically employ a language model trained to either generate individual proof steps ([Lample et al., 2022](#bib.bibx10); [Polu et al., 2022](#bib.bibx20); [Deepmind, 2024](#bib.bibx3); [Wang, 2023](#bib.bibx24); [Wu et al., 2024](#bib.bibx28); [Xin et al., 2025](#bib.bibx30); [Lin et al., 2025a](#bib.bibx15); [Wang et al., 2024](#bib.bibx25)) or produce entire proof completions from a given proof state ([Xin et al., 2024](#bib.bibx29); [Lin et al., 2025a](#bib.bibx15); [First et al., 2023](#bib.bibx5); [Huang et al., 2024](#bib.bibx6); [Wang et al., 2023](#bib.bibx26); [Jiang, 2023](#bib.bibx7)).\n\n  Line 445: A few recent works have attempted to integrate informal reasoning with formal theorem proving. For instance, [Lin et al., 2025](#bib.bibx14) proposed generating informal thoughts prior to predicting individual tactics, and [Wang et al., 2025](#bib.bibx27) employs a structured interaction between informal natural language reasoning and formal verification in Lean 4. However, these previous attempts either relied on conventional short-form chain-of-thought reasoning or relied on reasoning models trained via transfer learning from general reasoning tasks and supervised fine-tuning rather than reinforcement learning. Prior to our work, the feasibility and effectiveness of applying RL-driven long-form reasoning directly within formal theorem proving remained unexplored.\n\n  Line 451: The informal dataset serves as the foundational layer of our pipeline, feeding directly into supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and reinforcement learning (RL) for the large formal reasoning model. Below, we outline the data processing steps that convert the raw informal mathematical dataset into a curated prompt set for training our models.\n\n  Line 453: Our informal data pipeline consists of the following steps:\n\n  Line 497: Since the reward signal in this task is inherently fuzzy, and LLM-based feedback is susceptible to false positives, we monitor model outputs throughout training with Lean 4 experts and gradually improve the judge prompt over iterations. This allows us to maintain fine-grained control over the data quality. As our proving infrastructure improves, we also introduce automated filters to discard problematic formalizations. These include detecting logical contradictions, proving the negation of the formalized statement, or identifying that the problem is trivial via a short LLM-generated proof. These safeguards help ensure the training set remains both challenging and correct, guiding the model toward meaningful improvements in future iterations.\n\n  Line 507: We evaluate several existing models for autoformalization tasks, but found that most struggled to generate valid Lean 4 code consistently. Instead, these models predominantly produced Lean 3 syntax, a limitation we attribute to the composition of their pretraining data. Prior to these LLMs’ knowledge cutoff dates, Lean 3 dominated online repositories and documentation, while Lean 4—with its substantial syntax differences—remained relatively new and underrepresented. This training data imbalance caused models without specific Lean 4 fine-tuning to default to the more familiar Lean 3 patterns. The inability of comparison models to produce compilable Lean 4 code at meaningful rates ultimately prevented us from establishing fair benchmarks against existing approaches, highlighting the challenges of working with emerging formal languages.\n\n  Line 865: Kimina-Prover also excels at carrying out long calculations using the Lean 4 tactic calc. This is shown in the proof of aime\\_1997\\_p9 presented below. We would especially like to draw attention to the calc blocks required to prove statements h32, h33, ..., h34. in this proof. These calculation steps require careful and precise consideration of the current proof context and cannot rely solely on Lean 4’s automation tactics, which shows deep understanding of the formal theorem proving domain.\n\n  Line 873: Output: <think> \\# Solving for the 21st Term of an Arithmetic Sequence I’m given that in an arithmetic sequence, the 7th term is 30, and the 11th term is 60. I need to find the 21st term. First, let’s think about the structure of an arithmetic sequence. The general term of an arithmetic sequence is given by an\\=a+(n−1)​da\\_{n}=a+(n-1)d, where aa is the first term and dd is the common difference. Given: \\- The 7th term a7\\=a+6​d\\=30a\\_{7}=a+6d=30 \\- The 11th term a11\\=a+10​d\\=60a\\_{11}=a+10d=60 \\- We need to find the 21st term a21\\=a+20​da\\_{21}=a+20d Let’s solve for aa and dd to find a21a\\_{21}. Subtracting the first equation from the second gives: (a+10​d)−(a+6​d)\\=60−30(a+10d)-(a+6d)=60-30 4​d\\=304d=30 d\\=304\\=7.5d=\\\\frac{30}{4}=7.5 or 152\\\\frac{15}{2} Now, substituting d\\=7.5d=7.5 back into the first equation: a+6​(7.5)\\=30a+6(7.5)=30 a+45\\=30a+45=30 a\\=30−45\\=−15a=30-45=-15 So, the 21st term is a21\\=a+20​d\\=−15+20​(7.5)\\=−15+150\\=135a\\_{21}=a+20d=-15+20(7.5)=-15+150=135 Let’s formalize this in Lean 4: [⬇](data:text/plain;base64,aW1wb3J0IE1hdGhsaWIKaW1wb3J0IEFlc29wCgpzZXRfb3B0aW9uIG1heEhlYXJ0YmVhdHMgMAoKb3BlbiBCaWdPcGVyYXRvcnMgUmVhbCBOYXQgVG9wb2xvZ3kgUmF0CgoKdGhlb3JlbSBtYXRoZF9hbGdlYnJhXzM1NCAoYSBkIDog4oSdKSAoaOKCgCA6IGEgKyA2ICogZCA9IDMwKSAoaOKCgSA6IGEgKyAxMCAqIGQgPSA2MCkgOgogICAgYSArIDIwICogZCA9IDEzNSA6PSBieQ==) import Mathlib import Aesop set\\_option maxHeartbeats 0 open BigOperators Real Nat Topology Rat theorem mathd\\_algebra\\_354 (a d : ℝ) (h₀ : a + 6 \\* d \\= 30) (h₁ : a + 10 \\* d \\= 60) : a + 20 \\* d \\= 135 := by We have two equations: 1\\. a+6​d\\=30a+6d=30 (h0h\\_{0}) 2\\. a+10​d\\=60a+10d=60 (h1h\\_{1}) We need to find a+20​da+20d. From the two equations, we can eliminate aa to solve for dd. Subtracting h0h\\_{0} from h1h\\_{1}: (a+10​d)−(a+6​d)\\=60−30(a+10d)-(a+6d)=60-30 4​d\\=304d=30 d\\=30/4\\=7.5d=30/4=7.5 or 152\\\\frac{15}{2} Let’s compute this in Lean: [⬇](data:text/plain;base64,aGF2ZSBoZCA6IGQgPSAxNSAvIDIgOj0gYnkKICBsaW5hcml0aA==) have hd : d \\= 15 / 2 := by ...\n  Line 879: We observe that Kimina-Prover generates proofs by decomposing the argument into a sequence of ‘have‘ statements, resulting in a clear and highly readable logical structure. In contrast, the proof produced by BFS-Prover—which relies on step-by-step tactic search—makes it difficult to discern the purpose of each step directly from the code. This comparison highlights the strong proof sketch planning capabilities of Kimina-Prover.\n\n  Line 993: In addressing IMO 1983 P6, a challenging inequality problem, Kimina-Prover starts its analysis by exploring multiple variable substitution methodologies.\n\n  Line 1019: The reasoning contains a logical gap when trying to prove x2≤4x^{2}\\\\leq 4. Although correctly showing that x\\=±3x=\\\\pm 3 and x\\=±4x=\\\\pm 4 lead to non-integer values for yy, it fails to address whether larger values of xx could satisfy the equation. The model mistakenly assumed that solving the cases for n\\=3n=3 and n\\=4n=4 would resolve all cases for n\\>2n>2. This incomplete case analysis pattern hinders the model from solving problems correctly.\n\n  Line 1021: Experimental support, please [view the build logs](./2504.11354v1/__stdout.txt) for errors. Generated by [L A T E xml ![[LOGO]](data:image/png;base64,)](https://math.nist.gov/~BMiller/LaTeXML/)  .\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14d3773d7001gWdv6cbvfRnCrf:\n  Line 21: [![NCBI home page](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/pd-medc-pmc-cloudpmc-viewer/production/9b1d9d87/var/data/static/img/ncbi-logos/nih-nlm-ncbi--white.svg)](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/)\n\n  Line 73: ![Royal Society of Chemistry logo](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/banners/logo-rscsd.png)\n\n  Line 81: -   [View in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=\"Digit%20Discov\"[Title%20Abbreviation])\n\n  Line 158: Thermophysical properties such as the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of organic molecules are important to the performance of liquid formulations such as lubricants and refrigerants.[1](#cit1) Accurate prediction of these properties for pure organic molecules is also important for a wide range of industries, including energy, chemicals, and pharmaceuticals.[2–4](#cit2) In this context, the lack of reliable thermophysical data is one of the largest stumbling blocks in rational molecular,[5](#cit5) formulation,[6](#cit6) and process[7](#cit7) design. In particular, lubricants are required to adhere to strict viscosity limits across a wide temperature range (40–100 °C);[8](#cit8) however, for the organic molecules relevant to this application (C10–C50),[9](#cit9) viscosity–temperature data are still lacking. Viscosity data are available for many normal alkanes up to ∼C24, but data for branched alkanes, cycloalkanes, aromatics, alkyl cycloalkanes, and alkyl aromatics is much more sparse.[10](#cit10) There is also a lack of reliable thermophysical data available for oxygen-containing molecules such as esters, ethers, and glycols in the C10–C50 range.[11](#cit11) It is often possible to interpolate between missing entries with calculated predictions using a reliable equation of state, such as the perturbed-chain statistical associating fluid theory (PC-SAFT).[12,13](#cit12) Several other predictive models are available for pure hydrocarbons,[14](#cit14) with the most promising being group contribution and particularly entropy scaling methods.[15,16](#cit15) Quantitative structure–property relationship (QSPR) models[17–21](#cit17) have also been identified that are capable of predicting the viscosity of organic liquids. Within the chemical families and conditions for which they are parameterized, these methods can be highly accurate; their main limitation is reduced transferability, with predictive performance degrading outside the molecules and conditions fo...\n  Line 162: Machine learning (ML) has also emerged as a useful tool for predicting the thermophysical properties of pure organic liquids[43](#cit43) and their mixtures.[44](#cit44) Accurate viscosity prediction has been achieved using ML models such as artificial neural networks for single-component,[45–51](#cit45) binary mixtures,[52](#cit52) and multi-component[53,54](#cit53) organic liquids. ML methods have also been shown to be effective in predicting the viscosity of more complex molecules, such as ionic liquids[55](#cit55) and deep eutectic solvents.[56](#cit56) As with QSPR models, the performance of ML models remains at the mercy of accurate training data availability, with small, sparse or noisy datasets all having adverse effects on model accuracy. Even the largest available datasets on the thermophysical properties of fluids, such as the NIST REFPROP database,[57](#cit57) are sparse and subject to considerable uncertainties compared to other popular applications of ML.[43](#cit43)\n\n  Line 166: In this study, we benchmark ML models for the prediction of the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of lubricant-relevant organic liquids, and use MD simulations on a representative subset as an independent, physics-based assessment of the same properties. The primary aim is to develop ML models suitable for high-throughput virtual screening[62,68](#cit62) and molecular discovery[5,69](#cit5) workflows for high-performance lubricants. The MD simulations serve a complementary purpose, probing where and why physics-based prediction succeeds or fails for these types of molecules.\n\n  Line 170: ## Methodology\n\n  Line 174: To construct a robust and diverse dataset for model development and evaluation, we collected reference data for density, thermal conductivity and dynamic viscosity at atmospheric pressure and at both 40 °C and 100 °C. Only molecules with a dynamic viscosity of approximately 1–50 mPa s at 40 °C were included, because this is the range accessible to equilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations.[39](#cit39) Data were compiled from the Landolt–Börnstein series[73](#cit73) and the NIST Chemistry WebBook[74](#cit74) (accessed June 2024), resulting in 835 unique molecules carrying approximately 4700 critically evaluated reference values after the structure canonicalization, unit conversion and deduplication steps described below.\n\n  Line 176: In cases where the properties of interest were not provided exactly at 40 °C or 100 °C, interpolation was used to obtain the values at these temperatures. The NIST WebBook[74](#cit74) utilizes a combination of property estimation methods to interpolate liquid viscosity values from the available experimental data. These include the PPDS9 (Physical Property Data Service) and ViscosityL correlations, which are based on semi-empirical and theoretical equations of state (extended Andrade models[75,76](#cit75)) fitted from experimental values.[4](#cit4) The source method of every label is recorded, and the number of labels obtained by direct measurement *versus* by each database's own correlation or reference–property model is reported by property and temperature in Table S2. Most of the labels for density and thermal conductivity (99.0% and 97.8% at 40 °C) and approximately 44% of the viscosity labels are values produced by the source databases' own correlation, interpolation or reference–property models rather than direct measurements. The errors reported in this work therefore measure agreement with these adopted reference values, and do not include the uncertainty of the underlying database correlations. For molecules where both a direct measurement and a correlation value are available, the two agree closely across the full viscosity range at both temperatures (Fig. S1).\n\n  Line 178: For interpolation of the liquid density values, the NIST WebBook uses the Tait equation.[77](#cit77) For liquid thermal conductivity, the TransportPolynomial model was applied, utilizing temperature-dependent polynomial fits to experimental thermal conductivity data. For the Landolt–Börnstein series,[73](#cit73) cubic spline interpolation using a minimum of three surrounding data points was employed to estimate the property values at the desired temperatures. In cases where only the kinematic viscosity was reported, it was converted to dynamic viscosity using *µ* = *ν* × *ρ*, where *µ* is dynamic viscosity, *ν* is kinematic viscosity, and *ρ* is density. Molecules whose viscosity was reported only as kinematic and for which no density was available at the same temperature were excluded (283 molecules at each temperature). Canonicalization and deduplication of the 1187 source entries give 1114 unique molecules, of which 835 retain at least one usable label and form the dataset used here. The viscosity values were normalized by taking their natural logarithm to improve regression performance during ML training.[54](#cit54)\n\n  Line 260: Molecular fingerprints are typically represented as 2D binary strings, where each bit indicates the presence or absence of a specific structural feature or substructure within a molecule. A widely used and generally high-performing fingerprint type is the extended-connectivity fingerprint (ECFP), with the Morgan fingerprint being a common example.[82](#cit82) In this study, ECFPs are generated using the Morgan Fingerprint implementation in RDKit.[79](#cit79) While prior work has examined optimal ECFP parameters for molecular similarity assessment,[83](#cit83) no studies have specifically focused on optimizing ECFP generation for the property prediction of fuel- and lubricant-sized organic molecules using ML models. Accordingly, this study evaluates a range of ECFP generation parameters to identify those that yield the best regression performance. A systematic analysis of fingerprint parameters, including radius, bit size, and hashed *versus* unhashed representations,[83](#cit83) is provided in the SI.\n\n  Line 268: In this study, we evaluate a range of models using the collated dataset to identify the most suitable algorithm for property prediction. The classic ML models, including support vector machines (SVMs),[92](#cit92) random forests (RFs),[93](#cit93) and *k*\\-nearest neighbors (*k*\\-NN),[94](#cit94) are implemented using the scikit-learn package in Python.[95](#cit95) XGBoost (Extreme Gradient Boosting) models were trained using the XGBoost Python library.[96](#cit96) The graph-based models, message passing neural networks (MPNNs)[97](#cit97) and graph attention networks (GATs),[98](#cit98) are implemented with the PyTorch Geometric library.[99](#cit99) The deep learning models, convolutional neural networks (CNNs),[100](#cit100) multilayer perceptrons,[101](#cit101) and transformers[87](#cit87) were implemented with the PyTorch library.[99](#cit99) ChemBERTa[90,91](#cit90) was evaluated in two configurations using the HuggingFace Transformers library: fine-tuning the full pretrained encoder end-to-end on SMILES, and the frozen-embedding configuration described above, in which only a small MLP head is trained on the fixed mean-pooled encoder output.\n\n  Line 270: All models were evaluated under a single shared nested cross-validation protocol, applied to molecular representation configurations that were fixed in advance. The embedding dimensionality and the fingerprint radius and bit size were chosen by grid search on a single 80 : 10 : 10 train/validation/test split, as reported in the SI, so the protocol is nested with respect to hyperparameter selection and conditional on those representation configurations. The dataset was partitioned into five fixed outer folds, so that both temperatures of a given molecule always fall in the same fold and no molecule appears in more than one outer test set. The folds were constructed by stratified sampling over quintiles of the log-transformed 40 °C viscosity labels (molecules without a viscosity label were assigned *via* median-filled strata), then frozen. For each outer fold, hyperparameters were selected by random search over 25 configurations sampled from model-specific ranges, scored on a designated validation partition of the non-test data (approximately 85 : 15 train/validation); the selected configuration was then retrained on all non-test data and evaluated once on the untouched outer test fold. Full details of the architectures, hyperparameter ranges, and training procedures for each model are provided in the SI.\n\n  Line 274: The molecular structures of the eight molecules selected for this benchmarking study are shown in [Fig. 2](#fig2). These molecules were chosen to span the principal chemical families encountered in the reference dataset used in this work. All the MD simulations were performed using the LAMMPS software.[102](#cit102) EMD simulations were employed to calculate the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of the eight target molecules at 40 °C and 100 °C, consistent with conditions used to determine lubricant viscosity index values.[8](#cit8) All simulations were performed using the LOPLS-AA force field.[103](#cit103) Parameters for the LOPLS simulations were taken from Jorgensen *et al.*[104](#cit104) and Siu *et al.*[103](#cit103) Where required, the ester extension to LOPLS-AA was employed,[105](#cit105) while aromatic and ether parameters were taken from the corresponding OPLS-AA parameterizations. LOPLS-AA was selected because previous benchmarks have demonstrated it to be among the most accurate all-atom force fields for reproducing the experimental density and viscosity of linear hydrocarbons, including *n*\\-hexadecane,[28](#cit28)*n*\\-dodecane, and *n*\\-octacosane.[29](#cit29)\n\n  Line 332: XGBoost and RF models, when used with molecular descriptors, performed most consistently across all three transport properties. XGBoost with descriptors gave the lowest viscosity error of any combination (RMSE = 0.35 and 0.30 in ln(*η*/mPa s) at 40 and 100 °C, *R*2 = 0.86 and 0.83) and was among the best two or three combinations for density and thermal conductivity, while RF with descriptors gave the lowest density error (RMSE = 0.047 g cm−3). An error of 0.35 on the logarithmic scale corresponds to a typical discrepancy of a factor of 1.4 between predicted and measured viscosity. This aligns with previous studies,[124,125](#cit124) which also found tree-based models to be reliable across datasets. These methods handle nonlinear relationships and high-dimensional features effectively, making them suitable for the diverse information captured by molecular descriptors, and are relatively insensitive to feature scale, irregular target functions and uninformative features.[126](#cit126)\n\n  Line 354: It is well-known that ML models yield unreliable property predictions for molecules that lie outside the chemical domain of their training sets.[133,134](#cit133) This limitation poses significant challenges for molecular discovery pipelines,[135](#cit135) where candidate compounds frequently occupy regions of chemical space not sampled during training, and experimental measurements for such molecules are scarce. The severity of the problem depends on representation. Fingerprint-based models encode only substructures seen during training, so novel fragments activate no informative bits, while string-based tokenizations rely on sequential relationships that out-of-distribution atoms and functional groups distort. Molecular descriptors vary smoothly with structural modification and encode continuous global quantities that can change more gradually than sparse substructure indicators, making them comparatively better suited to extrapolation.[136](#cit136) Tossou *et al.* characterized out-of-distribution (OOD) prediction as a covariate shift problem and showed that performance degradation depends more on representation than on choice of model, with RDKit descriptors minimizing it.[137](#cit137)\n\n  Line 438: We also investigated the OOD prediction capabilities of the ML models, finding that extrapolation beyond the target property range seen during training degrades performance more than encountering molecules with novel atom types. This highlights the importance of training set coverage in both chemical and property space for reliable ML-based screening. The distance and predicted-value signals identify higher-risk regions at the population level rather than ordering the errors of individual molecules, and the two-signal rule remains exploratory because it was assessed on the same external set used to formulate it. We have demonstrated the ability of ML models to accurately predict the thermophysical properties of lubricant base oil-sized molecules, provided that they are trained on high-quality critically evaluated reference data for around a thousand unique molecules, which supports their use as fast property evaluators within multi-objective molecular design workflows for lubricants and other advanced formulations.\n\n  Line 452: E.O. thanks the Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (EPSRC) for PhD funding through their Doctoral Training Partnership (DTP) scheme (EP/T51780X/1). D.D. thanks the Royal Academy of Engineering (RAEng) for support through the Shell/RAEng Research Chair in Complex Engineering Interfaces. J.P.E. thanks the RAEng for support through their Research Fellowships scheme. We also acknowledge support from the Shell University Technology Centre (UTC) for Mobility and Lubricants and the EPSRC InFUSE Prosperity Partnership (EP/V038044/1). We acknowledge the use of Imperial College London Research Computing Service ([https://doi.org/10.14469/hpc/2232](https://doi.org/10.14469/hpc/2232)) and the UK Materials and Molecular Modelling Hub, which is partially funded by the EPSRC (EP/T022213/1, EP/W032260/1 and EP/P020194/1). We thank Stefan J. Eder (TU Wien) for useful discussions.\n\n  Line 456: The property values used to train and evaluate the machine learning models in this study were obtained from the NIST Chemistry WebBook[74](#cit74) and the Landolt–Börnstein series,[73](#cit73) and are redistributed with permission. The canonical dataset of 835 molecules (canonical SMILES, IUPAC name, the property labels at 40 °C and 100 °C, and per-label source provenance for every property), the fixed five molecule-level outer folds used for every benchmark in this work, the OOF predictions of all 26 model–representation combinations, their training and feature-generation code, the out-of-distribution test set together with the applicability-domain signals and per-molecule errors behind that analysis, the SMILES-to-LOPLS-AA system builder, LAMMPS input scripts, and Green–Kubo analysis script for the MD case study, and the conda environment specification are available at [https://github.com/fionn-carman/MD-ML-thermophysical](https://github.com/fionn-carman/MD-ML-thermophysical). The fitted model weights are archived on Zenodo ([https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21824121](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21824121)) owing to their size.\n\n  Line 488: 27.  Moore J. D. Cui S. T. Cochran H. D. Cummings P. T. Rheology of lubricant basestocks: A molecular dynamics study of C30 isomers. J. Chem. Phys. 2000;113:8833–8840. doi: 10.1063/1.1318768. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1318768)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=J.%20Chem.%20Phys.&title=Rheology%20of%20lubricant%20basestocks:%20A%20molecular%20dynamics%20study%20of%20C30%20isomers&author=J.%20D.%20Moore&author=S.%20T.%20Cui&author=H.%20D.%20Cochran&author=P.%20T.%20Cummings&volume=113&publication_year=2000&pages=8833-8840&doi=10.1063/1.1318768&)\\]\n\n  Line 506: 45.  Homer J. Generalis S. C. Robson J. H. Artificial neural networks for the prediction of liquid viscosity, density, heat of vaporization, boiling point and Pitzer's acentric factor. Part I. Hydrocarbons. Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys. 1999;1:4075–4081. doi: 10.1039/A904096J. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1039/A904096J)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=Phys.%20Chem.%20Chem.%20Phys.&title=Artificial%20neural%20networks%20for%20the%20prediction%20of%20liquid%20viscosity,%20density,%20heat%20of%20vaporization,%20boiling%20point%20and%20Pitzer's%20acentric%20factor.%20Part%20I.%20Hydrocarbons&author=J.%20Homer&author=S.%20C.%20Generalis&author=J.%20H.%20Robson&volume=1&publication_year=1999&pages=4075-4081&doi=10.1039/A904096J&)\\]\n\n  Line 507: 46.  Suzuki T. Ebert R. U. Schüürmann G. Application of Neural Networks to Modeling and Estimating Temperature-Dependent Liquid Viscosity of Organic Compounds. J. Chem. Inf. Comput. Sci. 2001;41:776–790. doi: 10.1021/ci000154y. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1021/ci000154y)\\] \\[[PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11410058/)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=J.%20Chem.%20Inf.%20Comput.%20Sci.&title=Application%20of%20Neural%20Networks%20to%20Modeling%20and%20Estimating%20Temperature-Dependent%20Liquid%20Viscosity%20of%20Organic%20Compounds&author=T.%20Suzuki&author=R.%20U.%20Ebert&author=G.%20Sch%C3%BC%C3%BCrmann&volume=41&publication_year=2001&pages=776-790&pmid=11410058&doi=10.1021/ci000154y&)\\]\n\n  Line 509: 48.  Santak P. Conduit G. Predicting physical properties of alkanes with neural networks. Fluid Phase Equilib. 2019;501:112259. doi: 10.1016/j.fluid.2019.112259. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fluid.2019.112259)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=Fluid%20Phase%20Equilib.&title=Predicting%20physical%20properties%20of%20alkanes%20with%20neural%20networks&author=P.%20Santak&author=G.%20Conduit&volume=501&publication_year=2019&pages=112259&doi=10.1016/j.fluid.2019.112259&)\\]\n\n  Line 511: 50.  Loh G. C. Lee H. C. Tee X. Y. Chow P. S. Zheng J. W. Viscosity Prediction of Lubricants by a General Feed-Forward Neural Network. J. Chem. Inf. Model. 2020;60:1224–1234. doi: 10.1021/acs.jcim.9b01068. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jcim.9b01068)\\] \\[[PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32058720/)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=J.%20Chem.%20Inf.%20Model.&title=Viscosity%20Prediction%20of%20Lubricants%20by%20a%20General%20Feed-Forward%20Neural%20Network&author=G.%20C.%20Loh&author=H.%20C.%20Lee&author=X.%20Y.%20Tee&author=P.%20S.%20Chow&author=J.%20W.%20Zheng&volume=60&publication_year=2020&pages=1224-1234&pmid=32058720&doi=10.1021/acs.jcim.9b01068&)\\]\n\n\n(Results truncated. Consider using a more specific path or pattern.)"
