sults indicate that the majority of structures generated by MatterGen are stable, and very close to the DFTlocal energy minimum. We further investigate whether MatterGen can generate a substantial amount of unique and novel materials. We showcase in [Fig. 2](#S2.F2 \"In 2.2 Generating stable, diverse materials ‣ 2 Results ‣ MatterGen: a generative model for inorganic materials design\")(d) that the percentage of unique structures is 100 % when generating 1...\n  Line 624: We employ an SE(3)-equivariant GNNto predict scores for the lattice, atom positions, and atom types in the denoising process. In particular, we adapt the GemNet architecture by [Gasteiger et al. (2021)](#bib.bib70), originally developed to be a universal MLFF. GemNet is a symmetric message-passing GNNthat uses directional information to achieve SO(3)-equivariance, and incorporates two- and three-body information in the first layer for efficiency. Since we do not predict energies, we adapt the direct (i.e., non-conservative) force prediction variant of the model, named GemNet-dT, which has been shown to be more computationally efficient and accurate in these scenarios [Gasteiger et al. (2021)](#bib.bib70). We employ four message-passing layers, a cutoff radius of 7 Å for the neighbor list construction, and set the dimension of hidden representations for nodes and edges to 512.\n\n  Line 678: which is scale-invariant and equivariant under rotation. The equivariance derives from the way it is composed with the Cartesian coordinate matrix, and the scale invariance is due to the normalization happening inside 𝚽~l\\\\tilde{{\\\\bm{\\\\Phi}}}^{l}. In particular, the diagonal entries of 𝚽~l\\\\tilde{{\\\\bm{\\\\Phi}}}^{l} related to the edges are normalized three times: they are divided by the total number of edges, and then multiplied twice by the inverse of the norm of the edge vectors. Given these properties, 𝒔^𝜽\\\\hat{{\\\\bm{s}}}\\_{{\\\\bm{\\\\theta}}} behaves like a symmetric stress tensor 𝝈{\\\\bm