ce the standard deviation of the long-time, noise-dominated tail. The thermal conductivity was then taken as the average of *κ*(*t*) over the window \\[*t*\\*, *t*\\* + 10 ps\\], immediately after the autocorrelation has decayed and before long-time noise sets in, with the reported value and uncertainty given by the mean and standard deviation of this window average across the 10 independent replicas.\n\n  Line 550: 89.  Rajan K. Zielesny A. Steinbeck C. STOUT: SMILES to IUPAC names using neural machine translation. J. Cheminf. 2021;13:34. doi: 10.1186/s13321-021-00512-4. \\[[DOI](https://doi.org/10.1186/s13321-021-00512-4)\\] \\[[PMC free article](/articles/PMC8077691/)\\] \\[[PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33906675/)\\] \\[[Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?journal=J.%20Cheminf.&title=STOUT:%20SMILES%20to%20IUPAC%20names%20using%20neural%20machine%20translation&author=K.%20Rajan&author=A.%20Zielesny&author=C.%20Steinbeck&volume=13&publication_year=2021&pages=34&pmid=33906675&doi=10.1186/s13321-021-00512-4&)\\]\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b8f9415001OxugAIIL4cotAW:\n  Line 1931: Training. Only low-rank adapters are trained; the base weights are frozen. For each adapted projection W0∈ℝdout×dinW\\_{0}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{d\\_{\\\\text{out}}\\\\times d\\_{\\\\text{in}}} (attention q,k,v,oq,k,v,o and MLP gate,up,down\\\\text{gate},\\\\text{up},\\\\text{down}), the effective weight is W\\=W0+(α/r)​B​AW=W\\_{0}+(\\\\alpha/r)\\\\,BA with A∈ℝr×dinA\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{r\\\\times d\\_{\\\\text{in}}}, B∈ℝdout×rB\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{d\\_{\\\\text{out}}\\\\times r}, rank r\\=32r=32, α\\=64\\\\alpha=64, and BB zero-initialized so training starts at the base model ([Hu et al., 2022](#bib.bib9)). We use AdamW, learning rate 5×10−55\\\\times 10^{-5}, cosine schedule with warmup ratio 0.030.03, two epochs, bf16, and gradient checkpointing. Remaining settings: per-device batch size 22 with gradient accumulation 88 (effective batch 1616), maximum sequence length 20482048 tokens (right-truncated, no packing), LoRA dropout 0.050.05, weight decay 00, gradient clipping at 1.01.0, trained on a single H100-80GB. The base verifier sees 2,5912{,}591 triples (1,8941{,}894 reference-equivalent / 697697 VPU); the mined verifier continue-trains from the prior best checkpoint under identical optimizer settings on those plus 732732 mined hard negatives. GenV+HN therefore sees 3,3233{,}323 examples, 1,8941{,}894 reference-equivalent and 1,4291{,}429 VPU, a 57:4357{:}43 class balance. The two-class reward-model heads add a single linear layer (22 logits over the 51205120\\-dim hidden state, ∼10​K\\\\sim 10K parameters) on top of the *same* adapter set, ∼200​M\\\\sim 200M trainable parameters; the generative readout adds no parameters at all, so the readout comparison is not confounded by capacity.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14c8a97a8001TiOs3p25aD48Si:\n  Line 123: We first perform a t-SNE analysis[36](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR36 \"van der Maaten, L. & Hinton, G. Visualizing data using t-sne. J. Mach. Learn. Res. 9, 2579–2605 (2008).\") for the molecular weight of the pre-training data set to speculate the effectiveness of Org-Mol pre-trained model in learning molecular structures and its ability to distinguish between different molecules. The 512-dimension embedding layer of the Org-Mol model is projected into two dimensions, and the distributions of the molecular weight of 20,000 randomly selected molecules in the pre-training data are shown in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)a and b. With the randomly initialized weight (as shown in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)a), the data points are more evenly distributed, while in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)b, where the Org-Mol pre-trained weight is applied, the data points with similar molecular weights tend to cluster together, elucidating that a simple structure-property relationship has been constructed by the Org-Mol pre-trained model. As detailed in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)c, to accurately predict physical properties of various organic compounds, we carry out the fine-tuning process for each physical property of interest so that a more specific and sophisticated structure-property relationship can be established. The size and range of each property are summarized in Table [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Tab1).\n\n  Line 628: [![Advertisement](//pubads.g.doubleclick.net/gampad/ad?iu=/285/npjcompumats.nature.com/article&sz=300x250&c=1951264776&t=pos%3Dright%26type%3Darticle%26artid%3Ds41524-025-01720-4%26doi%3D10.1038/s41524-025-01720-4%26subjmeta%3D1034,1037,301,403,563,638,639%26kwrd%3DOrganic+chemistry,Computational+methods,Theoretical+chemistry)](//pubads.g.doubleclick.net/gampad/jump?iu=/285/npjcompumats.nature.com/article&sz=300x250&c=1951264776&t=pos%3Dright%26type%3Darticle%26artid%3Ds41524-025-01720-4%26doi%3D10.1038/s41524-025-01720-4%26subjmeta%3D1034,1037,301,403,563,638,639%26kwrd%3DOrganic+chemistry,Computational+methods,Theoretical+chemistry)\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b686363001vHsApeDEyr3MVm:\n  Line 139: Lean Server. In our reinforcement learning and evaluation pipelines, we integrate the Numina Lean Server ([Numina, 2025](#bib.bibx18)) as the verification backend to provide real-time feedback for generated proof attempts. Built upon Lean FRO’s LeanREPL ([Lean FRO, 2023](#bib.bibx11)), the Numina Lean Server employs an LRU-based caching mechanism that reuses preloaded environments based on import headers, significantly reducing initialization overhead. Furthermore, it supports extensive parallelization across multiple CPUs by managing multiple Lean REPL processes concurrently. These innovations result in a 10×10\\\\times speedup in verification throughput, achieving up to 100 iterations per second on machines equipped with 64 CPU cores and 512 GB RAM. During RL training, this 