ely predict physical properties of various organic compounds, we carry out the fine-tuning process for each physical property of interest so that a more specific and sophisticated structure-property relationship can be established. The size and range of each property are summarized in Table [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Tab1).\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14be0ff55001Ah1Jt4GLsVC1KW:\n  Line 220: The introduction of Li in place of Na remarkably boosted the ionic conductivity by more than two orders of magnitude, specifically at a substitution level of one third, exemplified by Na2LiYCl6 (x\\=2x=2) exhibiting a conductivity of 2.2×10−62.2\\\\times 10^{-6} S/cm at 100 °C, compared to the meager 6.0×10−86.0\\\\times 10^{-8} S/cm at 100 °C observed in the parent Na3YCl6 material. The measured ionic conductivities (Figure [5](#S2.F5 \"Figure 5 ‣ 2.6.1 Structure and composition ‣ 2.6 Synthesis and experimental characterization ‣ 2 Large-scale materials discovery ‣ Accelerating computational materials discovery with artificial intelligence and cloud high-performance computing: from large-scale screening to experimental validation\")c) across varying xx values consistently depict the rising trend associated with increased Li content. This trend strongly implies that the heightened mobility of Li+ ions actively contributes to the measured conductivity within these substituted compositions. Simultaneously, a significant reduction of activation energy (0.46–0.66 eV) was observed with the introduction of lithium (x\\=x= 0.5 to 3) compared to the parent Na3YCl6 (0.82 eV). Notably, Na2LiYCl6 (with a similar R3¯\\\\bar{3} crystal structure as parent Na3YCl6, as shown in Figure [5](#S2.F5 \"Figure 5 ‣ 2.6.1 Structure and composition ‣ 2.6 Synthesis and experimental characterization ‣ 2 Large-scale materials discovery ‣ Accelerating computational materials discovery with artificial intelligence and cloud high-performance computing: from large-scale screening to experimental validation\")d) displays a significantly lower activation energy (Ea\\=E\\_{a}= 0.6 eV), indicating an amplified ionic diffusion process.\n\n  Line 453: x\\=2x=2\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14c823a3c001PG071G2NmVOA1r:\n  Line 103: We first perform a t-SNE analysis[Van der Maaten and Hinton (2008)](#bib.bib43) for the molecular weight of the pre-training data set to speculate the effectiveness of Org-Mol pre-trained model in learning molecular structures and its ability to distinguish between different molecules. The 512-dimension embedding layer of the Org-Mol model is projected into two dimensions, and the distributions of the molecular weight of 20,000 randomly selected molecules in the pre-training data are shown in Figure [1](#S2.F1 \"Figure 1 ‣ 2 Computational Details ‣ High-Accuracy Physical Property Prediction for Organics via Molecular Representation Learning: Bridging Data to Discovery\")𝐛\\\\bf b and 𝐜\\\\bf c. With the randomly initialized weight (as shown in Figure [1](#S2.F1 \"Figure 1 ‣ 2 Computational Details ‣ High-Accuracy Physical Property Prediction for Organics via Molecular Representation Learning: Bridging Data to Discovery\")𝐛\\\\bf b), the data points are more evenly distributed, while in Figure [1](#S2.F1 \"Figure 1 ‣ 2 Computational Details ‣ High-Accuracy Physical Property Prediction for Organics via Molecular Representation Learning: Bridging Data to Discovery\")𝐜\\\\bf c where the Org-Mol pre-trained weight is applied, the data points with similar molecular weights tend to cluster together, elucidating that a simple structure-property relationship has been constructed by the Org-Mol pre-trained model. As detailed below, to accurately predict physical properties of various organic compounds, we carry out the fine-tuning process for each physical property of interest so that a more specific and sophisticated structure-property relationship can be established.\n\n  Line 119: While the static dielectric constant is a bulk property, its value for various organic compounds can be accurately predicted via the Org-Mol fine-tuned model with only the single molecular coordinates as the input. As shown in Figure [2](#S3.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Prediction of the electrical property ‣ 3 Results and discussion ‣ High-Accuracy Physical Property Prediction for Organics via Molecular Representation Learning: Bridging Data to Discovery\")𝐛\\\\bf b, for the static dielectric constant at the room temperature, the R2R^{2} value of the training set (over 850 organic liquids) is 0.990, while the R2R^{2} value of the test set is around 0.968, with an overall MAE 0.726 (as shown in Figure [2](#S3.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Prediction of the electrical property ‣ 3 Results and discussion ‣ High-Accuracy Physical Property Prediction for Organics via Molecular Representation Learning: Bridging Data to Discovery\")𝐜\\\\bf c). Such high accuracy lies in the fact that tremendous molecular structures are fed to the pre-trained model, and the corresponding structural characteristics are efficiently captured by the final 512 descriptors via a 15-layer transformer-based neural network. Therefore, the Org-Mol fine-tuned model can be applied for an effective screening tool to filter out potential candidates for immersion coolants with desired low dielectric constant.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14cafec09001IyUywC2yFsf1I7:\n  Line 742:                             IntroductionOver the last few years, several machine-learned weather prediction models have emerged that show a higher level of skill than traditional physics-based models in a variety of forecast scores.The first generation of these models produced deterministic predictions and were trained to minimise a mean-squared-error (MSE) loss1,2,3,4,5. The MSE training objective incentivises the smoothing of forecast fields with lead-time and a reduction of forecast activity, to avoid the ’double-penalty’ incurred when forecasting misplaced structures (e.g., refs. 6,7). Nevertheless, evaluations demonstrated that these models display surprisingly physical behaviour in classical forecast situations8, and provide useful predictions, including of many extreme events9. The European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts (ECMWF) is now producing operational weather predictions using the Artificial Intelligence Forecasting System (AIFS,5) four times per day.For the usefulness of a weather forecast, it is important to account for forecast uncertainties. Ensemble forecasts are run to estimate the probability density of the atmospheric state at a future time10,11. In physics-based numerical weather prediction (NWP), this is achieved via running the forecast model from a range of perturbed initial conditions and by introducing stochastic perturbations into the forecast model itself. The aim is to generate a well-calibrated ensemble. This means that on average, the ensemble standard deviation needs to match the root-mean square error of the ensemble mean (e.g., ref. 12), and the predicted probability of an event should accurately reflect the observed probability of it occurring.For physics-based ensemble simulations with the Integrated Forecasting System (IFS) at ECMWF13, initial condition uncertainty is represented via an ensemble of data assimilations14,15,16 and singular vector perturbations11. Perturbations to the initial conditions of the ensemble m...\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b6a88bf001FdC1IrKy62u1zP:\n  Line 316: In our reinforcement learning and evaluation pipelines, we integrate the Numina Lean Server (Numina, 2025) as the verification backend to provide real-time feedback for generated proof attempts. Built upon Lean FRO’s LeanREPL (Lean FRO, 2023), the Numina Lean Server employs an LRU-based caching mechanism that reuses preloaded environments based on import headers, significantly reducing initialization overhead. Furthermore, it supports extensive parallelization across multiple CPUs by managing multiple Lean REPL processes concurrently. These innovations result in a 10×10\\times speedup in verification throughput, achieving up to 100 iterations per second on machines equipped with 64 CPU cores and 512 GB RAM. During RL training, this verification system operates efficiently in the rollout phase, evaluating proofs in real time as they are generated. Due to the relatively slow proof-generation process, verification does not become a bottleneck, requiring only 640 CPU cores for training. This efficiency contrasts with previous language model-based theorem proving approaches, which typically require thousands of CPU cores to sustain real-time verification at scale (Lample et al., 2022; Xin et al., 2024).\n\n  Line 364: 102400\n\n  Line 385: 1024\n\n  Line 396: 1024\n\n  Line 410: 1024\n\n  Line 1802: =y3​z+x​y3−x​y2​z−x2​y2+2​y2​z2+2​x​y2​z−2​x​y​z2−2​x2​y​z+y​z3+x​y​z2−x​z3−x2​z2=y^{3}z+xy^{3}-xy^{2}z-x^{2}y^{2}+2y^{2}z^{2}+2xy^{2}z-2xyz^{2}-2x^{2}yz+yz^{3}+xyz^{2}-xz^{3}-x^{2}z^{2}\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14bf129ce001zIa3E9O7wQVcER:\n  Line 341: We further introduce adapter modules to enable fine-tuning towards any given property constraints with a labeled dataset. Compared to prior generative models, structures produced by MatterGen are more than twice as likely to be novel and stable, and more than 15 times closer to the local energy minimum. After fine-tuning, MatterGen successfully generates stable, novel materials with desired chemistry, symmetry, as well as mechanical, electronic and magnetic properties.\n\n  Line 409: To assess stability, we perform DFTcalculations on 1024 generated structures.\n\n  Line 1191: We employ four message-passing layers, a cutoff radius of 7 Å for the neighbor list construction, and set the dimension of hidden representations for nodes and edges to 512.\n\n  Line 1285: In particular, the diagonal entries of 𝚽~l\\tilde{{\\bm{\\Phi}}}^{l} related to the edges are normalized three times: they are divided by the total number of edges, and then multiplied twice by the inverse of the norm of the edge vectors.\n\n  Line 1723: The plots displayed in Fig. 2(b,c,e,f), and the structures showed in Fig. 2(1) refer to samples of 1024 generated structures which have also been relaxed via DFT.\n\n  Line 1730: Within the 1024 structures generated unconditionally for Fig. 2(a), a total of 43 unique, on-hull crystal structures were found: 11 binaries, 22 ternaries, and 10 quaternaries. These are summarized in Table 2. Of these, 3 had P1 symmetry, and 3 contained molecules or were molecular crystals. Prototype assignment limited to prototypes available in the robocrystallographer Ganose and\n\n  Line 2053: Table 2: Summary information on 43 crystal structures that were calculated as being thermodynamically stable from a batch of 1024 crystal structures from an unconditional generation task.\n\n  Line 2242: For each property in Fig. 5(a-c), we generate 512512 samples with our fine-tuned model by conditioning on a value of 0.2​Å−30.2~\\text{\\AA}^{-3} for magnetic density, 3.03.0 eV for band gap, and 400400 GPa for bulk modulus. We relax those structures using the MLFFand filter the relaxed structures by stability and uniqueness. We then relax the remaining structures with DFTand filter by stability, uniqueness, and novelty. Finally, we compute the desired property of the remaining structures using DFT, and filter out structures where we consider the computed property to be an outlier, i.e., high bulk modulus values with more than 600 GPa and magnetic density values with more than 0.3Å−3~\\text{\\AA}^{-3}. For more details on the DFTcalculations, see Section C.2. After all filters and DFTcomputations, we obtain 251 S.U.N.structures for magnetic density, 142 for band gap, and 22 for bulk modulus.\n\n  Line 2301: To evaluate the performance of our model, we proceed as detailed in Section D.6, and generate 512 samples with our fine-tuned model, by conditioning on a magnetic density value of 0.2 Å−3\\text{\\AA}^{-3} and an HHIscore of 1200.\n\n  Line 3956: 10238–10249\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14d1bdd8c001i3hG07GXheoqgC:\n  Line 123: We first perform a t-SNE analysis[36](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#ref-CR36 \"van der Maaten, L. & Hinton, G. Visualizing data using t-sne. J. Mach. Learn. Res. 9, 2579–2605 (2008).\") for the molecular weight of the pre-training data set to speculate the effectiveness of Org-Mol pre-trained model in learning molecular structures and its ability to distinguish between different molecules. The 512-dimension embedding layer of the Org-Mol model is projected into two dimensions, and the distributions of the molecular weight of 20,000 randomly selected molecules in the pre-training data are shown in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)a and b. With the randomly initialized weight (as shown in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)a), the data points are more evenly distributed, while in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)b, where the Org-Mol pre-trained weight is applied, the data points with similar molecular weights tend to cluster together, elucidating that a simple structure-property relationship has been constructed by the Org-Mol pre-trained model. As detailed in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)c, to accurately predict physical properties of various organic compounds, we carry out the fine-tuning process for each physical property of interest so that a more specific and sophisticated structure-property relationship can be established. The size and range of each property are summarized in Table [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Tab1).\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14d394719001zhZuBuqrxpIxDr:\n  Line 1061: As with the viscosity, the thermal conductivity was obtained from the running Green–Kubo integral κ(t), with the heat-flux ACF averaged over the three Cartesian components and evaluated by FFT.118 Unlike the shear stress, however, the heat-flux ACF carries no slow tail. The ACF decays to zero within a few picoseconds, so the running integral κ(t) reaches an early plateau and then drifts as statistical noise accumulates in the long-time tail. The double-exponential TDM fit used for the viscosity is therefore not required for the thermal conductivity, and integrating κ(t) to long lag times would only accumulate noise; the uncertainty of the Green–Kubo estimate grows with the upper integration limit relative to the total trajectory length,39 so the conductivity must be read from the short-time plateau rather than the converged integral. To locate this plateau without a hand-chosen cutoff, we first identified the lag time t* at which the normalized heat-flux ACF decays into its noise floor, defined as the first point beyond which its magnitude remains below twice the standard deviation of the long-time, noise-dominated tail. The thermal conductivity was then taken as the average of κ(t) over the window [t*, t* + 10 ps], immediately after the autocorrelation has decayed and before long-time noise sets in, with the reported value and uncertainty given by the mean and standard deviation of this window average across the 10 independent replicas.Results and discussion\n\n  Line 1308: Rajan K. Zielesny A. Steinbeck C. STOUT: SMILES to IUPAC names using neural machine translation. J. Cheminf. 2021;13:34. doi: 10.1186/s13321-021-00512-4. [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14d49eb0e001aEMu3vWXpuBXVb:\n  Line 1061: As with the viscosity, the thermal conductivity was obtained from the running Green–Kubo integral κ(t), with the heat-flux ACF averaged over the three Cartesian components and evaluated by FFT.118 Unlike the shear stress, however, the heat-flux ACF carries no slow tail. The ACF decays to zero within a few picoseconds, so the running integral κ(t) reaches an early plateau and then 