g data are shown in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)a and b. With the randomly initialized weight (as shown in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)a), the data points are more evenly distributed, while in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)b, where the Org-Mol pre-trained weight is applied, the data points with similar molecular weights tend to cluster together, elucidating that a simple structure-property relationship has been constructed by the Org-Mol pre-trained model. As detailed in Fig. [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Fig1)c, to accurately predict physical properties of various organic compounds, we carry out the fine-tuning process for each physical property of interest so that a more specific and sophisticated structure-property relationship can be established. The size and range of each property are summarized in Table [1](/articles/s41524-025-01720-4#Tab1).\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14bdf786f001n3uOxcT5JsJnUc:\n  Line 86: For the fine-tuning process for TinyLlama-1.1B models in our experimental configuration, we refined our model’s fine-tuning process with selected hyperparameters encapsulated within the TrainingArguments setup. This configuration specified an output directory, a per-device train batch size of 8 (due to memory constraints) and applied gradient accumulation over 4 steps to efficiently balance computational demand and memory constraints. The model optimization was conducted using paged_adamw_32bit with a learning rate set at 5 × 10−4, and a cosine learning rate scheduler was employed for optimal learning rate adjustments throughout the training phase. A save strategy based on epochs was utilized, coupled with logging and evaluation intervals set at 25 and 50 steps, respectively, aligning with an evaluation strategy that triggers at specified steps to closely monitor the model’s performance. The training was streamlined to complete within 1 epoch to ensure quick adaptation while preventing overfitting, without setting a maximum step limit and avoiding mixed precision training to maintain computational accuracy. The SFTTrainer was used in the training process, directly interfacing with the training and validation datasets, and was configured with peft_config for tailored pre-fine-tuning adjustments. This allowed us to set our specified hyperparameters and training configurations. Text preprocessing was managed using a specified dataset_text_field and tokenizer, with packing disabled and a maximum sequence length of 512 to standardize input data handling. This approach aimed at enhancing the model’s learning efficiency, prioritizing a balance between optimizing the computational resources and achieving high-quality model training.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b9a68ba001BMxHuiZFcGnqct:\n  Line 1931: Training. Only low-rank adapters are trained; the base weights are frozen. For each adapted projection W0∈ℝdout×dinW\\_{0}\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{d\\_{\\\\text{out}}\\\\times d\\_{\\\\text{in}}} (attention q,k,v,oq,k,v,o and MLP gate,up,down\\\\text{gate},\\\\text{up},\\\\text{down}), the effective weight is W\\=W0+(α/r)​B​AW=W\\_{0}+(\\\\alpha/r)\\\\,BA with A∈ℝr×dinA\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{r\\\\times d\\_{\\\\text{in}}}, B∈ℝdout×rB\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^{d\\_{\\\\text{out}}\\\\times r}, rank r\\=32r=32, α\\=64\\\\alpha=64, and BB zero-initialized so training starts at the base model ([Hu et al., 2022](#bib.bib9)). We use AdamW, learning rate 5×10−55\\\\times 10^{-5}, cosine schedule with warmup ratio 0.030.03, two epochs, bf16, and gradient checkpointing. Remaining settings: per-device batch size 22 with gradient accumulation 88 (effective batch 1616), maximum sequence length 20482048 tokens (right-truncated, no packing), LoRA dropout 0.050.05, weight decay 00, gradient clipping at 1.01.0, trained on a single H100-80GB. The base verifier sees 2,5912{,}591 triples (1,8941{,}894 reference-equivalent / 697697 VPU); the mined verifier continue-trains from the prior best checkpoint under identical optimizer settings on those plus 732732 mined hard negatives. GenV+HN therefore sees 3,3233{,}323 examples, 1,8941{,}894 reference-equivalent and 1,4291{,}429 VPU, a 57:4357{:}43 class balance. The two-class reward-model heads add a single linear layer (22 logits over the 51205120\\-dim hidden state, ∼10​K\\\\sim 10K parameters) on top of the *same* adapter set, ∼200​M\\\\sim 200M trainable parameters; the generative readout adds no parameters at all, so the readout comparison is not confounded by capacity.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14bf038f0001VQe1H3chANjAFy:\n  Line 310: The ANI (Accurate NeurAl networK engINe for Molecular Energies) family of models (e.g., ANI-1 [18], ANI-1x [19], ANI-1ccx [20], ANI-2x [21]) proposes a different approach, where feed forward neural networks are trained on handcrafted Atomic Environment Vectors (AEVs, adapted from the basis functions proposed by Behler and Parrinello [10]) to predict molecular energies and forces at near-DFT accuracy (and at times near Coupled Cluster accuracy [20]. While specialized for small organic molecules, ANI models have been showed to offer good transferability and computational efficiency, and have placed themselves as a reference benchmark for multiple specialized applications at times outperforming DFT (B3LYP) and OPLS [22].\n\n  Line 1326: Comparing ani-2x, ani-1ccx neural networks, force field, and dft methods for predicting conformational potential energy of organic molecules.\n\n  Line 1686: ISSN 1520-5126.\n\n  Line 1991: 64x0e + 64x0o + 32x1e + 32x1o + 4x2e + 4x2o\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14bd98e8e00192YR4sclD16to6:\n  Line 1101: CoRR, abs/1512.03385, 2015.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14d3410db001fQsqCS1rO77HBf:\n  Line 1061: As with the viscosity, the thermal conductivity was obtained from the running Green–Kubo integral κ(t), with the heat-flux ACF averaged over the three Cartesian components and evaluated by FFT.118 Unlike the shear stress, however, the heat-flux ACF carries no slow tail. The ACF decays to zero within a few picoseconds, so the running integral κ(t) reaches an early plateau and then drifts as statistical noise accumulates in the long-time tail. The double-exponential TDM fit used for the viscosity is therefore not required for the thermal conductivity, and integrating κ(t) to long lag times would only accumulate noise; the uncertainty of the Green–Kubo estimate grows with the upper integration limit relative to the total trajectory length,39 so the conductivity must be read from the short-time plateau rather than the converged integral. To locate this plateau without a hand-chosen cutoff, we first identified the lag time t* at which the normalized heat-flux ACF decays into its noise floor, defined as the first point beyond which its magnitude remains below twice the standard deviation of the long-time, noise-dominated tail. The thermal conductivity was then taken as the average of κ(t) over the window [t*, t* + 10 ps], immediately after the autocorrelation has decayed and before long-time noise sets in, with the reported value and uncertainty given by the mean and standard deviation of this window average across the 10 independent replicas.Results and discussion\n\n  Line 1308: Rajan K. Zielesny A. Steinbeck C. STOUT: SMILES to IUPAC names using neural machine translation. J. Cheminf. 2021;13:34. doi: 10.1186/s13321-021-00512-4. [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14be0fef1001nugnsaw5dAW2Q3:\n  Line 704: -   \\[24\\] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, and Jian Sun. Deep residual learning for image recognition. CoRR, abs/1512.03385, 2015.\n\n\n(Results truncated. Consider using a more specific path or pattern.)"
